through the last three years. when it was
an international call. I would answer it.

4
00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:50.883
And... it was a reporter in Dublin. um...
wanted a reaction for the story.

5
00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.720
I said. "Reaction for what?"

6
00:00:53.721 --> 00:00:57.424
And then they were really caught
back by that question obviously.

7
00:00:57.425 --> 00:01:04.637
and they said "Uh. I'll call you back in five
minutes". And so then I went online and...

8
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:08.141
<i>saw the picture.</i>

9
00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:15.675
It's not the way you want to find out.

10
00:01:15.676 --> 00:01:21.854
I never. ever imagined that... that it
would end in that fashion. right?

11
00:01:24.785 --> 00:01:31.798
<i>For days. that first week. I just would
constantly see that image and...</i>

12
00:01:32.626 --> 00:01:35.828
<font color=#ff00ff>[Sighs]</font><i> It's been. uh... it's been tough.</i>

13
00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:40.500
You go through those emotional swings and.
you know. why the hell did you go back.

14
00:01:40.501 --> 00:01:43.503
you know that second time? And then...

15
00:01:43.504 --> 00:01:46.212
But I keep coming back to the same answer.

16
00:02:15.102 --> 00:02:19.571
<i>Name the sports newspaper that
hit US newsstands in 1990.</i>

17
00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:20.272
<font color=#ff00ff>- [Buzzer Buzzing]</font>
- <i>James.</i>

18
00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:22.649
- The National.
- <i>It was.</i>

19
00:02:59.713 --> 00:03:01.421
<font color=#ff00ff>[Camera Clicking]</font>

20
00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:07.820
<i><font color=#FF551>MAN:</font> Ladies and gentlemen. please
help me welcome. Mr. James Foley.</i>

21
00:03:07.821 --> 00:03:10.263
<font color=#ff00ff>[Audience Applauding]</font>

22
00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:21.000
Thanks for that generous... overly
generous. uh... introduction.

23
00:03:21.001 --> 00:03:27.607
And I'm definitely not a hero or
noble or anything. I'm just. uh...

24
00:03:27.608 --> 00:03:31.878
Trying to do my work and... uh...
got into a little bit of trouble.

25
00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:33.920
<font color=#ff00ff>[Chanting]</font>

26
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.153
<i>I arrived in Benghazi in mid
March. and the night I arrived.</i>

27
00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:45.692
- <i>I heard nothing but bombs and gunfire.</i>
<font color=#ff00ff>- [Bombs Exploding]</font>

28
00:03:45.693 --> 00:03:48.961
<i>I wondered. "What's going
on?" Another journalist said.</i>

29
00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:52.939
"The bombs. that's gelatina.
that's how they fish."

30
00:03:54.635 --> 00:03:59.838
Blow the fish up. "And. uh... the gunfire.
no. that's celebratory gunfire."

31
00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.513
<font color=#ff00ff>[Rapid Gunfire]</font>

32
00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:07.587
<i>If Gadhafi comes to Benghazi.
are you guys prepared to fight?</i>

33
00:04:21.862 --> 00:04:25.832
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> I think in some ways.
Libya was a turning point for Jim.</i>

34
00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:28.200
You know. I was starting to see his

35
00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:32.103
trying to figure out where
he belonged in the world.

36
00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:35.355
<i>You know he tried the
Teach for America. and...</i>

37
00:04:35.356 --> 00:04:39.010
<i>then in Chicago. he worked
at the Cook County Jail.</i>

38
00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:43.615
<i>He anguished over his teaching. He didn't
think he was a good enough teacher.</i>

39
00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:48.220
<i>And he wanted to write and he
loved people and liked to interact.</i>

40
00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:54.126
tell people stories. So when Jim decided he
wanted to go into journalism from teaching.

41
00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:58.661
at first we thought. "That would be good.
maybe that'll be a better fit. Jim."

42
00:04:58.662 --> 00:05:02.734
But when he decided to do
conflict journalism. uh...

43
00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:05.436
<font color=#ff00ff>[Sighs]</font> you know. that was
a whole different deal.

44
00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:09.140
Well. he didn't exactly tell us. <font color=#ff00ff>[Chuckles]</font>

45
00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:14.213
<i>He um... graduated from Medill. and we
said "Jim. well. what're you gonna do?"</i>

46
00:05:14.214 --> 00:05:16.915
And he said. "Well. I'm... I'm working on it."

47
00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:23.930
So the next thing we knew. he was gonna go to
Iraq embedded with the Indiana National Guard.

48
00:05:25.959 --> 00:05:32.098
Well. I guess I'm a conflict journalist at
this point. so that's how we found out.

49
00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:34.967
<font color=#ff00ff>[Guns Firing]</font>

50
00:05:34.968 --> 00:05:39.846
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> It's an all out sprint. an exhilarating
and dangerous run for cover.</i>

51
00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.373
Got 'em.

52
00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:47.620
When Jim started the journalism path. we
did have some heated discussions on...

53
00:05:48.415 --> 00:05:51.784
views and... and the military and what not.

54
00:05:51.785 --> 00:05:55.587
<i>My views are a little bit more conservative...
<font color=#ff00ff>[Chuckles]</font> than Jim's.</i>

55
00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:58.190
<i>I would say a little bit more liberal.</i>

56
00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:04.230
<i>But when he did his embed. I think.
is where we came closer together.</i>

57
00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:09.999
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> As soon as the shooting died down. the
platoon discovered a body limp on the roof.</i>

58
00:06:12.139 --> 00:06:17.709
- <i>Do they know who the guy is?</i>
- Yes. These are the family members.

59
00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.986
<i><font color=#FF551>- JIM:</font> These are the family?
- Yeah.</i>

60
00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:33.525
<i><font color=#FF551>JOHN JR.:</font> And he came back to my
house and he told me... he was like.</i>

61
00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:37.804
"Hey. John. I'm thinking about going to Libya."

62
00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:47.406
I'm like. "Jim. that's a horrible idea.
That is absolutely a horrible idea.

63
00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:53.512
If you go over there... no one's coming for you.

64
00:06:53.513 --> 00:06:57.116
<i>Why would you put your life in danger?
We're dropping bombs over there.</i>

65
00:06:57.117 --> 00:07:03.222
And God forbid if you go over there
and we accidentally kill you.

66
00:07:03.223 --> 00:07:10.162
I mean. why? Wh... why?

67
00:07:10.163 --> 00:07:12.999
<font color=#ff00ff>[Chanting]</font>

68
00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.702
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Libya was very exciting as a journalist
because you had this chance</i> to talk directly

69
00:07:16.703 --> 00:07:19.070
to the people. to see exactly what was going on.

70
00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:20.739
<font color=#ff00ff>[Chanting Indistinctly]</font>

71
00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.810
<i>There was no U.S. soldiers anymore.
no organized army anymore.</i>

72
00:07:23.811 --> 00:07:26.578
<i>telling you what you should and shouldn't do.</i>

73
00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:29.956
But it was also. uh... extremely dangerous.

74
00:07:32.119 --> 00:07:36.556
I was actually talking to one of my buddies.
Bostey about my brother Jim. and how.

75
00:07:36.557 --> 00:07:39.358
you know. he's this journalist
and is like super crazy.

76
00:07:39.359 --> 00:07:42.661
but like badass at the same time.
you know? I was like. yeah.

77
00:07:42.662 --> 00:07:46.297
<i>my brother's a badass. you know?
He's crazy. he's awesome.</i>

78
00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:49.201
I also was very naive myself. I didn't
know exactly what that would mean.

79
00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:53.104
I didn't know he was actually gonna be
immersed in actual. like. crossfire.

80
00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:56.342
There's snipers in this building
about one kilometer away.

81
00:07:56.343 --> 00:07:59.645
Tank. The groups decided to go in.

82
00:07:59.646 --> 00:08:06.191
Little bit of heavy fighting. Heavy casualties.
about 30 wounded. two dead out of the 40.

83
00:08:08.155 --> 00:08:14.926
There's one video that always stands out to
me. It's like when he's standing in the square.

84
00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:19.498
There does seem to be a strong sense
that they won't give up the fight

85
00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:21.968
<i>and that there are a force
of young fighting men.</i>

86
00:08:21.969 --> 00:08:27.573
although unorganized. there's
plenty of will to hold out here.

87
00:08:27.574 --> 00:08:31.543
How does my guy know this stuff?
Like. how does Jim Foley. like...

88
00:08:31.544 --> 00:08:34.080
<i>He's just my meathead friend. you know?</i>

89
00:08:34.081 --> 00:08:38.417
A... a... and it was so foreign to
me in terms of an experience.

90
00:08:38.418 --> 00:08:40.719
I'm like. well. how do you get into Libya?

91
00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:44.290
Oh. you're a freelancer. like who
wants this? Are you getting paid?

92
00:08:44.291 --> 00:08:51.170
This is Jim Foley reporting from downtown
Benghazi. Revolutionary Square. Global Post.

93
00:08:51.932 --> 00:08:58.404
Jim was there at the early stage of this
movement of there being more freelancers

94
00:08:58.405 --> 00:09:00.547
in conflict areas.

95
00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:06.312
<i>The world has changed so much
in terms of digital publishing</i>

96
00:09:06.313 --> 00:09:13.185
and newspapers started to eliminate
things that they didn't see as essential.

97
00:09:13.186 --> 00:09:16.888
<i>International coverage dwindled
down to very little.</i>

98
00:09:16.889 --> 00:09:23.034
So we saw an opportunity to fill that void
and we needed to work with freelancers.

99
00:09:23.463 --> 00:09:31.469
Freelancers decide to work together just on
the basis of this. initial quick read chemistry.

100
00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:36.642
I saw this new guy who I hadn't met before.
He looked friendly enough. so I said.

101
00:09:36.643 --> 00:09:41.280
"Hey. what's up?" He said. "Oh. not
much. going to the front line".

102
00:09:41.281 --> 00:09:47.386
<i>And he'd heard a lot about Libya and
the fact that it was very cheap to work.</i>

103
00:09:47.387 --> 00:09:51.290
<i>Rebels and protesters were eager
to show us their side of the story.</i>

104
00:09:51.291 --> 00:09:57.296
<i>You know. they were driving us all over for
free. They were translating for us for free.</i>

105
00:09:57.297 --> 00:10:03.434
M... many of us never really experienced the
luxury of. um... journalism in its heyday.

106
00:10:03.435 --> 00:10:07.439
What we do is journalism
on a shoestring budget.

107
00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:13.445
So we've had to be a lot more resourceful
in a way and just more street savvy.

108
00:10:13.446 --> 00:10:19.685
I think in a sense. the way we all got to know
each other was the Africa hotel in Benghazi.

109
00:10:19.686 --> 00:10:24.763
It was the cheapest hotel and the
crappiest and we were all staying there.

110
00:10:32.165 --> 00:10:35.333
I'd seen Jim talking to. you
know. a few other journalists

111
00:10:35.334 --> 00:10:38.570
<i>and. um... he was just really
friendly with everybody.</i>

112
00:10:38.571 --> 00:10:42.541
<i>It was unusual in a place like that. You know.
there's still an edge of competitiveness</i>

113
00:10:42.542 --> 00:10:46.678
in that environment. whereas Jim
was just like. "Yeah. whatever".

114
00:10:46.679 --> 00:10:49.348
<i>He gave off a really good first
impression and. you know.</i>

115
00:10:49.349 --> 00:10:53.684
it helps that he's like a super good-looking
guy. and I was just like. "Who is this guy?

116
00:10:53.685 --> 00:10:55.120
Who are you?"

117
00:10:55.121 --> 00:10:58.089
Just that jaw man. like. just fucking
cut cheese with that thing.

118
00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:02.294
There wasn't anything mundane
about the man whatsoever.

119
00:11:02.295 --> 00:11:09.068
He never really like. projected himself onto
a situation and he dealt well with people.

120
00:11:09.069 --> 00:11:14.046
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Who are the Libyan rebels determined
to overthrow 42 years of dictatorship?</i>

121
00:11:19.645 --> 00:11:20.712
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Off-shore welding?</i>

122
00:11:20.713 --> 00:11:24.716
- Platform. yeah.
- <i>Okay. dangerous job.</i>

123
00:11:24.717 --> 00:11:30.261
<i>They're brave as individuals. but many show
a dangerous lack of weapons training.</i>

124
00:11:31.924 --> 00:11:34.260
<i><font color=#FF551>CLARE:</font> There were so many
freelancers who came in</i>

125
00:11:34.261 --> 00:11:36.828
<i>at that point in time who were so new.</i>

126
00:11:36.829 --> 00:11:41.733
Jim and I among them. and I found out
only later that there were a number

127
00:11:41.734 --> 00:11:47.639
of much more experienced photo-journalists
who'd made one or two trips to the front line

128
00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:51.117
and said "Fuck it. this is way too dangerous".

129
00:12:33.186 --> 00:12:36.261
Jim had a high tolerance for danger.

130
00:12:36.856 --> 00:12:38.033
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Shit!</i>

131
00:12:38.034 --> 00:12:41.527
I mean. sure. he was drawn
to that. All of us are in a way.

132
00:12:41.528 --> 00:12:44.996
He was cool as a cucumber as well.
Hey. like wearing this little like.

133
00:12:44.997 --> 00:12:47.733
<i>tiny vest that he used to wear.</i>

134
00:12:47.734 --> 00:12:54.906
<i><font color=#FF551>CLARE:</font> The fact that he stayed so calm made
it easy to feel calm in that situation. but.</i>

135
00:12:54.907 --> 00:13:00.385
of course. sometimes. I was just like. "Well.
that's crazy. I'm not going there with you".

136
00:13:02.482 --> 00:13:06.285
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> I would come back to Benghazi
and there was stuff going on there.</i>

137
00:13:06.286 --> 00:13:10.789
<i>There was families and they
were out there in the protest.</i>

138
00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:13.525
<i>and they were out there maybe
handling medical supplies.</i>

139
00:13:13.526 --> 00:13:18.764
which is probably more important to what
this revolutionary movement was...

140
00:13:18.765 --> 00:13:24.743
<i>but then being called out to that front
line again like some kind of siren song.</i>

141
00:13:28.741 --> 00:13:32.511
<i>It was one of those mornings where we
decided we were gonna get out there early.</i>

142
00:13:32.512 --> 00:13:34.946
<i>We wanted to get a fresh
look at the front lines.</i>

143
00:13:34.947 --> 00:13:39.551
<i>It was myself. Clare Gillis. Manu
Brabo and Anton Hammerl.</i>

144
00:13:39.552 --> 00:13:41.187
<i>a South African photo-journalist.</i>

145
00:13:41.188 --> 00:13:44.690
- <i>Meet my new friend. Anton.</i>
- Anton.

146
00:13:44.691 --> 00:13:49.195
<i>And what it was really was just a highway.
a coastal highway going all across Libya.</i>

147
00:13:49.196 --> 00:13:51.597
<i>and this is where the battle was going on.</i>

148
00:13:51.598 --> 00:13:55.907
<i>So it was kind of like a Mad Max type war.</i>

149
00:13:57.303 --> 00:13:59.571
<i>Now this was something common
that some reporters did.</i>

150
00:13:59.572 --> 00:14:04.075
<i>Freelancers like myself didn't have big
budgets. we'd jump in with the rebels.</i>

151
00:14:04.076 --> 00:14:08.186
<i>And it was at your own risk if
you wanted to go further or not.</i>

152
00:14:09.616 --> 00:14:12.684
<i>We got to the points where we
saw another group of rebels</i>

153
00:14:12.685 --> 00:14:15.921
<i>saying Gadhafi forces are 300 meters away.</i>

154
00:14:15.922 --> 00:14:20.191
And myself looking at Clare.
like. that's impossible.

155
00:14:20.192 --> 00:14:25.564
<i>And I remember. you know. Anton turning
to me and saying "Hey. this isn't safe".</i>

156
00:14:25.565 --> 00:14:32.238
<i>But we didn't turn around. and we said.
"Well. let's get off the road anyways".</i>

157
00:14:32.239 --> 00:14:35.607
Well. that was the exact wrong thing to do.

158
00:14:35.608 --> 00:14:41.753
<i>Two heavily armed Gadhafi pickup
trucks came over that rise firing.</i>

159
00:14:43.616 --> 00:14:46.318
<i>I remember so clearly the sound of it.</i>

160
00:14:46.319 --> 00:14:51.757
<i>the volume of it. the sound
of something eating metal...</i>

161
00:14:51.758 --> 00:14:57.295
<i>and I remember hoping against hope that
there would be some kind of out. out of this.</i>

162
00:14:57.296 --> 00:15:01.873
<i>there was some kind of trap door in time.</i>

163
00:15:05.071 --> 00:15:09.975
<i>I crawled back to the sand dune. Anton
was at the other sand dune in front of me.</i>

164
00:15:09.976 --> 00:15:11.883
<i>I heard him call for help.</i>

165
00:15:13.646 --> 00:15:16.848
<i>It appeared he was cut across
the midsection with AK fire</i>

166
00:15:16.849 --> 00:15:20.186
<i>and it was a serious amount of blood.
He had already lost consciousness</i>

167
00:15:20.187 --> 00:15:23.128
<i>and probably already died.</i>

168
00:15:26.559 --> 00:15:32.198
<i>A group of young soldiers approached me
and we were thrown in the back of a truck.</i>

169
00:15:32.199 --> 00:15:36.568
I remember getting photographed
with a cell phone.

170
00:15:36.569 --> 00:15:39.805
and thinking. you know. this is where
they find all these photographs

171
00:15:39.806 --> 00:15:46.351
<i>that are evidence of war crimes some
day. and realizing this is me now.</i>

172
00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:55.554
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> I was with my mother.</i>

173
00:15:55.555 --> 00:15:58.856
<i>We were out to lunch and
I received a phone call.</i>

174
00:15:58.857 --> 00:16:03.128
and. um. that's how we first heard. you know?

175
00:16:03.129 --> 00:16:08.907
I think I was in denial about how
dangerous this really was. Brian.

176
00:16:09.602 --> 00:16:15.507
I was furious. just furious.

177
00:16:15.508 --> 00:16:17.809
Scared for him. furious.

178
00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:22.848
I hate to revisit it. but it's
just like... I told you. Jim.

179
00:16:22.849 --> 00:16:29.821
I think we all went through the stages of
total shock. you know. and then just...

180
00:16:29.822 --> 00:16:35.991
What are we gonna do. and then anger.
After all we're family. you know?

181
00:16:39.231 --> 00:16:43.068
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> You're so humble. You lost everything.</i>

182
00:16:43.069 --> 00:16:49.007
<i>your freedom. your control. your ability to talk
to anybody and tell anybody you're okay.</i>

183
00:16:49.008 --> 00:16:52.644
<i>thinking one minute. "Oh. yeah.
I'm a foreign correspondent.</i>

184
00:16:52.645 --> 00:16:59.291
<i>and the next minute somebody who you
respect killed. and you have nothing."</i>

185
00:17:03.222 --> 00:17:07.926
<i><font color=#FF551>CLARE:</font> Jim was concerned
that his own competitiveness</i>

186
00:17:07.927 --> 00:17:14.900
with Manu. with Anton. with himself. his
own. sort of macho aggressiveness

187
00:17:14.901 --> 00:17:19.237
had driven him to make decisions
that were not the best decisions.

188
00:17:19.238 --> 00:17:21.540
We were all questioning our judgment.

189
00:17:21.541 --> 00:17:27.678
<i>One of the main things that affected
all of us is that Anton had kids</i>

190
00:17:27.679 --> 00:17:29.921
<i>and none of us do.</i>

191
00:17:30.817 --> 00:17:35.487
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Every day I have to deal
with the fact that Anton is not</i>

192
00:17:35.488 --> 00:17:37.689
<i>going to ever see his three kids anymore.</i>

193
00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:43.995
and I was part of that decision-making
process... that took him away.

194
00:17:43.996 --> 00:17:48.073
that took him away from his kids. and his wife.

195
00:18:02.582 --> 00:18:05.183
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> And I had a lot of time
to play over those moments.</i>

196
00:18:05.184 --> 00:18:08.354
<i>especially that one day when we were captured.</i>

197
00:18:08.355 --> 00:18:13.191
<i>I tried to question myself. "What are your
reporting on. what is this all about?"</i>

198
00:18:13.192 --> 00:18:16.996
<i>It was quickly apparent
that this was about being</i>

199
00:18:16.997 --> 00:18:19.931
<i>what you think is an authentic
conflict correspondent.</i>

200
00:18:19.932 --> 00:18:24.002
<i>seeing the front line and it not
being enough to just see it</i>

201
00:18:24.003 --> 00:18:27.072
<i>from a distance. but to
push it to the next level.</i>

202
00:18:27.073 --> 00:18:32.644
You were basically waiting to get
shelled and the question is. why?

203
00:18:32.645 --> 00:18:35.721
You know. why... why are you doing this?

204
00:18:38.685 --> 00:18:42.488
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> A lot of us were just scratching
our head. right? There's no money.</i>

205
00:18:42.489 --> 00:18:44.990
<i>Maybe you get a story here
or there that you sell.</i>

206
00:18:44.991 --> 00:18:48.126
You know I used to "loan him money".

207
00:18:48.127 --> 00:18:54.966
You know. my credit report has one ding on it
and it's the one loan that I co-signed with Jim.

208
00:18:54.967 --> 00:18:59.237
I think Jimmy was just a little
outside the lines. you know.

209
00:18:59.238 --> 00:19:01.540
Well. he was wicked disorganized.

210
00:19:01.541 --> 00:19:03.974
<i><font color=#FF551>MARK:</font> We had to tell him to come to dinner.</i>

211
00:19:03.975 --> 00:19:08.579
Like if dinner was at 5:00. we'd tell him
it was at 3:00. so he'd show up at 5:00.

212
00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.948
He always lives in the moment.

213
00:19:10.949 --> 00:19:14.185
<i>I don't know if Mike ever told you
that Jim was late to his wedding.</i>

214
00:19:14.186 --> 00:19:19.558
"John. can I sleep at your house?" "John.
can my friend stay at your house?"

215
00:19:19.559 --> 00:19:22.794
<i>"Yes. Jim. yes."</i>

216
00:19:22.795 --> 00:19:28.133
Even though you just were like. "Jim. just
wake up! Wake up! What are you doing?

217
00:19:28.134 --> 00:19:31.637
<i>Get a real job. start saving up for retirement."</i>

218
00:19:31.638 --> 00:19:36.441
I remember I said like. you know. why don't
you come to Chicago. my... my dad runs this

219
00:19:36.442 --> 00:19:39.077
<i>boot camp. it's teaching young felons.</i>

220
00:19:39.078 --> 00:19:42.080
He goes there for the interview and
I call him afterwards and I'm like.

221
00:19:42.081 --> 00:19:46.117
"How'd the interview go?" He's like "Oh.
it was great. I think it went great".

222
00:19:46.118 --> 00:19:49.354
I call up my dad. and he goes.
"He said it went great. did he?

223
00:19:49.355 --> 00:19:53.124
'Cause I just talked to the lady at the hiring
board and when she went out there.

224
00:19:53.125 --> 00:19:56.928
she found Jim sleeping with his head back
on a wall and he's wearing jeans with like

225
00:19:56.929 --> 00:20:00.731
paints or something all over them".
And I called Jim back. I'm like. Jim.

226
00:20:00.732 --> 00:20:01.933
"what the hell happened at the interview?"

227
00:20:01.934 --> 00:20:06.605
He's like. "It was hot in there. Tom. It was hot.
I just put my head back and I nodded off."

228
00:20:06.606 --> 00:20:09.140
And I'm like. "Did you have jeans
on with paint?" He's like.

229
00:20:09.141 --> 00:20:11.610
"Yeah. they had like these like. symbols
on it. what was I supposed to wear?"

230
00:20:11.611 --> 00:20:13.311
I'm like. "Jim. it's an interview.

231
00:20:13.312 --> 00:20:16.481
<i>it's an interview. You can't do this!"
And so my dad always says that.</i>

232
00:20:16.482 --> 00:20:20.985
"Jim has a million dollar resume
and a ten cent interview."

233
00:20:20.986 --> 00:20:25.323
<i>He came and he started teaching
reading and writing. some literature</i>

234
00:20:25.324 --> 00:20:29.127
and he was really good with these kids
'cause Jim was into hip-hop and rap and

235
00:20:29.128 --> 00:20:31.530
<i>he liked to grab a mic himself.</i>

236
00:20:31.531 --> 00:20:34.199
Jim connected with these guys. He'd
find out about their backgrounds.

237
00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:36.367
talk to them. I think that really hit him.

238
00:20:36.368 --> 00:20:40.471
Like he was seeing young guys that came
from terrible neighborhoods that...

239
00:20:40.472 --> 00:20:42.007
<i>never really had a chance.</i>

240
00:20:42.008 --> 00:20:46.351
<i>And I think he started seeing like. there
are people who need their stories told.</i>

241
00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:54.285
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> The prison that we were
transferred to was filled</i>

242
00:20:54.286 --> 00:20:57.222
<i>with all these political prisoners
from all walks of life.</i>

243
00:20:57.223 --> 00:20:59.224
<i>Some were fishermen. some were engineers.</i>

244
00:20:59.225 --> 00:21:03.461
It was the warmth of these Libyans that
said. "Here. you're our guest. take this bed.

245
00:21:03.462 --> 00:21:05.263
You don't have a cigarette.
here's a cigarette." you know.

246
00:21:05.264 --> 00:21:08.333
"You want an extra piece of chicken. here."

247
00:21:08.334 --> 00:21:12.137
<i>These guys understand that I'm a
journalist and I'm trying to get the truth</i>

248
00:21:12.138 --> 00:21:16.574
and perhaps wrongly they think I'm on their
side. but I certainly feel like it now.

249
00:21:16.575 --> 00:21:18.550
<font color=#ff00ff>[Chanting Prayers On PA]</font>

250
00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:27.352
<i>You know that if... if you are kinda without
hope. you're totally humbled.</i>

251
00:21:27.353 --> 00:21:30.855
<i>you tend to go to your faith if you have faith.</i>

252
00:21:30.856 --> 00:21:36.862
<i>Praying five times a day. it was the life.
it was their only source of life in prison.</i>

253
00:21:36.863 --> 00:21:39.564
<i>I was up nights talking to them. Eventually.
you know. one of them said.</i>

254
00:21:39.565 --> 00:21:41.399
<i>"Why don't you pray with us?"</i>

255
00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:45.736
And I said. "No. I don't pray to east. I pray
to Jesus." when you guys are praying.

256
00:21:45.737 --> 00:21:48.373
He sort of looked at me. like. "Okay".

257
00:21:48.374 --> 00:21:53.552
<i>Well. I guess I'm experiential person
so I started praying with them.</i>

258
00:21:56.248 --> 00:21:59.917
Well. little did I know I just
proclaimed my conversion to Islam.

259
00:21:59.918 --> 00:22:01.486
<font color=#ff00ff>[Audience Laughing]</font>

260
00:22:01.487 --> 00:22:05.590
<i>So from then on out. I prayed
with them five times a day</i>

261
00:22:05.591 --> 00:22:11.296
<i>and... and it was just... it was so powerful
and it was something that I needed to do.</i>

262
00:22:11.297 --> 00:22:15.233
But it was difficult. I was thinking. you
know. "Jesus. am I praying to Allah?"

263
00:22:15.234 --> 00:22:18.836
"Am I violating my belief in you?"

264
00:22:18.837 --> 00:22:20.771
<i>I don't have an answer to that.</i>

265
00:22:20.772 --> 00:22:23.241
<i>I just know that I was authentically
praying with them and I was</i>

266
00:22:23.242 --> 00:22:25.851
<i>authentically also praying to Jesus.</i>

267
00:22:29.816 --> 00:22:32.851
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> Jim and I were very close in age.</i>

268
00:22:32.852 --> 00:22:36.922
<i>looked enough alike that you
know. I used his ID in college.</i>

269
00:22:36.923 --> 00:22:39.958
<i>but that's about it. We
couldn't be more different.</i>

270
00:22:39.959 --> 00:22:43.729
I mean. Jim was really different
from... from most of us. right?

271
00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:48.366
I mean. to do what he went on
to do. It's not a normal path.

272
00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:53.371
<i>When Jim was taken in Libya. I just
went to overdrive at that point.</i>

273
00:22:53.372 --> 00:22:57.802
It's a nightmare. Each day. it becomes harder
you know. with... with the lack of information.

274
00:22:57.803 --> 00:22:59.545
We know he's in Tripoli.

275
00:22:59.546 --> 00:23:04.983
<i>We believe he's in a detention center. We
really don't know much more beyond that.</i>

276
00:23:04.984 --> 00:23:10.188
We organized this huge group of Jimmy's
friends. we called them FOJ's. Friends of Jim.

277
00:23:10.189 --> 00:23:13.659
<i>We had a lot of outside help. but Michael
was sort of the CEO of the group.</i>

278
00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:18.896
His statement was. you know. "There are no
immeasurables other than getting Jim home".

279
00:23:18.897 --> 00:23:23.935
Our biggest fear is that it becomes
yesterday's story and people forget about it.

280
00:23:23.936 --> 00:23:28.480
<i>We love Jim and we miss
him. We want him home.</i>

281
00:23:30.442 --> 00:23:34.520
<i><font color=#FF551>BRIAN:</font> Tell me about the release.
what was that day like for you guys?</i>

282
00:23:36.348 --> 00:23:38.583
<i><font color=#FF551>REPORTER:</font> After successful
diplomatic negotiations.</i>

283
00:23:38.584 --> 00:23:41.920
<i>two American journalists and a Spanish
journalist are finally going home</i>

284
00:23:41.921 --> 00:23:45.857
<i>after being kidnapped and detained by
the Libyan government forces for 44 days.</i>

285
00:23:45.858 --> 00:23:47.692
- What's your name?
- Manu Brabo.

286
00:23:47.693 --> 00:23:50.228
- Are you okay?
- I'm fine.

287
00:23:50.729 --> 00:23:53.231
If you didn't catch it the first time
around. get it from your buddy.

288
00:23:53.232 --> 00:23:58.236
Guys. could you please give them some space.

289
00:23:58.237 --> 00:24:00.405
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> What was really interesting
about that whole process.</i>

290
00:24:00.406 --> 00:24:06.011
there was a day where we were making some
progress and I just decided that I have to go.

291
00:24:06.012 --> 00:24:08.045
I have to get on a plane and go.

292
00:24:08.046 --> 00:24:09.948
<i>I don't know. I can't... I can't describe it.</i>

293
00:24:09.949 --> 00:24:15.126
<i>I knew. at least felt strongly
that it was gonna work out.</i>

294
00:24:16.355 --> 00:24:18.523
<i>And all the security guys
wanted to debrief him first.</i>

295
00:24:18.524 --> 00:24:21.292
<i>and finally I said. "Enough of this shit"
and just went into the hotel room.</i>

296
00:24:21.293 --> 00:24:27.432
<i>and I remember seeing Jim. he had a full
beard. and he just grabbed me and said.</i>

297
00:24:27.433 --> 00:24:33.271
<i>"Mike." We both kinda just grabbed each
others arms like just to... is it real?</i>

298
00:24:33.272 --> 00:24:36.007
I ordered pizza and as many Heinekens

299
00:24:36.008 --> 00:24:38.343
as they could put on a card and
about two packs of cigarettes

300
00:24:38.344 --> 00:24:40.045
because I knew that's all they had over there.

301
00:24:40.046 --> 00:24:44.582
I looked at them and I said. "I
guess... I guess I got this one".

302
00:24:44.583 --> 00:24:47.425
<i>And we just stayed up all night.</i>

303
00:24:49.521 --> 00:24:54.365
<i><font color=#FF551>KATIE:</font> All the family and friends. we
were all together waiting for them...</i>

304
00:24:55.694 --> 00:25:01.900
<i>and all of a sudden we see these
blue lights just rushing at us.</i>

305
00:25:01.901 --> 00:25:07.005
We're like. "Oh. my God. this is Jim.
This is... he's actually here."

306
00:25:07.006 --> 00:25:11.850
<i>I don't know. it was like a movie. He
was just so happy to see everybody.</i>

307
00:25:26.725 --> 00:25:31.005
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> You have a close call. That's pure
luck that you didn't get killed there.</i>

308
00:25:31.006 --> 00:25:34.199
<i>It's not worth seeing your
mother or father bawling</i>

309
00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:38.069
<i>and worrying about your grandmother
dying because you're in prison.</i>

310
00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:40.578
<i>It's not... it's not worth these things.</i>

311
00:25:42.208 --> 00:25:46.111
And outside in my parent's
home in a comfortable house

312
00:25:46.112 --> 00:25:48.813
in New Hampshire. I sort
of had to start processing.

313
00:25:48.814 --> 00:25:52.817
<i>I was horrified to learn how much my
friends and family had done to help me.</i>

314
00:25:52.818 --> 00:25:54.519
<i>I was inspired and I was horrified.</i>

315
00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:58.529
It was a weird feeling of like going
to your own funeral. you know?

316
00:26:01.027 --> 00:26:04.535
<i>There's no going back from something like this.</i>

317
00:26:05.965 --> 00:26:10.508
<i>Some of the things that I'll never be able
to change. but I wish... I wish that I could.</i>

318
00:26:21.113 --> 00:26:25.156
This is Jim's blazer from when he
spoke at Marquette. this brown one.

319
00:26:27.319 --> 00:26:29.828
<i>Bunch of stuff to go through.</i>

320
00:26:31.023 --> 00:26:34.960
Yeah. so Jim lived with us for
what. three months? Summer...

321
00:26:34.961 --> 00:26:40.131
- Summer after he was released from Libya?
- Summer until... yeah. up until he went back.

322
00:26:40.132 --> 00:26:46.671
Um... he used to have all his clothes in here.
You can still see his Camelbak from the field.

323
00:26:46.672 --> 00:26:49.139
- <i>Sleeping bags.</i>
- All his sleeping bags.

324
00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:51.075
Yeah.

325
00:26:51.076 --> 00:26:56.014
That was his bed right there.
He'd always crash out there.

326
00:26:56.015 --> 00:27:01.086
Um... so it was cool having Jim here. especially
after him having been gone to Libya because...

327
00:27:01.087 --> 00:27:04.289
I think when he came back. you know...

328
00:27:04.290 --> 00:27:07.425
like you just wanted to
touch him a lot because...

329
00:27:07.426 --> 00:27:11.596
<i>I don't know. for me it was like. poke and
just make sure he's real and it was almost.</i>

330
00:27:11.597 --> 00:27:15.099
you're just more appreciative of
him and especially with the kids.

331
00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:19.570
you know. getting to have him here
because he was always on the go.

332
00:27:19.571 --> 00:27:23.333
They loved him. Uncle Jim. They'd come
downstairs at the crack of dawn and...

333
00:27:23.334 --> 00:27:25.110
<i>wake him up.</i>

334
00:27:25.111 --> 00:27:29.080
It was good as much as it... I
think he kind of like. you know.

335
00:27:29.081 --> 00:27:33.458
<i>had an itch he couldn't scratch
when he was domesticated.</i>

336
00:27:36.755 --> 00:27:42.393
When Jim came back from Libya. I offered
him a full-time job. uh... here as an editor

337
00:27:42.394 --> 00:27:47.365
and while he sorted out what he would do next.

338
00:27:47.366 --> 00:27:51.769
And he sat right outside my office.

339
00:27:51.770 --> 00:27:54.172
<i>He was grateful to have the job.</i>

340
00:27:54.173 --> 00:28:02.587
but working in an office was clearly not
um. what he... what he liked the most.

341
00:28:03.114 --> 00:28:07.552
<i>He was quiet. I don't want
to say he was withdrawn...</i>

342
00:28:07.553 --> 00:28:10.428
<i>but he... he was... he was quiet.</i>

343
00:28:11.723 --> 00:28:16.461
<i>And I just remember like. Brad my
fiancé and Jim were in the basement</i>

344
00:28:16.462 --> 00:28:21.699
and. um... Brad doesn't even remember
a time when Jim was sleeping.

345
00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:24.369
<i>You know. after the family
had kind of settled down.</i>

346
00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:27.372
<i>Jim just went right to business. right to work.</i>

347
00:28:27.373 --> 00:28:31.709
<i>We had found him a very good
psychologist to talk to.</i>

348
00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:37.148
but he seemed so "well". if you
will. um... that we didn't push it.

349
00:28:37.149 --> 00:28:41.727
But he was so restless here at home.
He didn't want to be here at home.

350
00:28:45.224 --> 00:28:47.058
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Feeling like you survived something.</i>

351
00:28:47.059 --> 00:28:52.262
<i>there's a strange sort of force that
you. uh... are drawn back to.</i>

352
00:28:52.263 --> 00:28:54.639
I think that's the absolute reality.

353
00:28:58.304 --> 00:29:02.107
Everything else becomes
foreign. Like. it was strange

354
00:29:02.108 --> 00:29:07.412
coming back to the real world. like
it was weird. It was normal in there.

355
00:29:07.413 --> 00:29:12.216
<i><font color=#FF551>CLARE:</font> Yeah. if I go to Wal-Mart and
I'm looking at. like literally an entire</i>

356
00:29:12.217 --> 00:29:16.694
aisle full of Tupperware. it boggles my mind.

357
00:29:17.456 --> 00:29:21.692
<i>There's a deep absurdity to understanding</i>

358
00:29:21.693 --> 00:29:26.731
the peace that we're lucky
enough to have in this country

359
00:29:26.732 --> 00:29:30.301
<i>and the questions that it raises about</i>

360
00:29:30.302 --> 00:29:36.541
<i>how do we make our money? What is the basis
of our peace. of our economic viability.</i>

361
00:29:36.542 --> 00:29:40.311
<i>of the fact that. you know. I can
look at all of this Tupperware</i>

362
00:29:40.312 --> 00:29:46.056
<i>and someone else. somewhere else is
looking at the ruins of their home?</i>

363
00:29:47.353 --> 00:29:50.821
<i><font color=#FF551>ZAC:</font> You know. you just start missing like all
the bombs and the fighting and everything.</i>

364
00:29:50.822 --> 00:29:54.159
<i>I just used to love sitting up at
like at night and just listening.</i>

365
00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:56.861
<font color=#ff00ff>[Guns Firing At Distance]</font>

366
00:29:56.862 --> 00:29:59.630
It's really kind of sick.

367
00:29:59.631 --> 00:30:04.301
<i>But it also... it feels like super
fortunate for being there.</i>

368
00:30:04.302 --> 00:30:07.104
And so you think like. out of 25
million people in your country.

369
00:30:07.105 --> 00:30:12.443
you're the only person who's got to experience
that. Like fuck man. like how fortunate am I?

370
00:30:12.444 --> 00:30:17.208
<i>Like. you're witnessing history unfolding
and you're just getting this perspective</i>

371
00:30:17.209 --> 00:30:20.858
<i>that is so unique.</i>

372
00:31:12.638 --> 00:31:15.940
I believe that front-line
journalism is important.

373
00:31:15.941 --> 00:31:22.547
Without these photos and videos and
firsthand experience. we can't really...

374
00:31:22.548 --> 00:31:28.159
- tell the world how bad it might be.
<font color=#ff00ff>- [Singing]</font>

375
00:31:31.823 --> 00:31:33.932
<font color=#ff00ff>[Bomb Blasting]</font>

376
00:31:41.166 --> 00:31:43.355
<i><font color=#FF551>REPORTER:</font> James Foley joins us now live</i>

377
00:31:43.356 --> 00:31:47.004
<i>from inside Northern Syria
with more on what he saw.</i>

378
00:31:47.005 --> 00:31:50.475
Tell us more about you were able...
What you were able to witness.

379
00:31:50.476 --> 00:31:54.479
Yes. thank you. You've heard about
indiscriminate shelling. but to see

380
00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:59.016
<i>those bodies left over from a direct
mortar hit was... was really shocking.</i>

381
00:31:59.017 --> 00:32:04.221
<i>They were civilians and they are
under pretty continuous shelling.</i>

382
00:32:04.222 --> 00:32:05.630
<font color=#ff00ff>[Chanting]</font>

383
00:32:23.375 --> 00:32:28.313
It seemed like he started
thinking about going to Syria

384
00:32:28.314 --> 00:32:31.048
and by the time he mentioned it.

385
00:32:31.049 --> 00:32:35.586
<i>it was like he'd already
kind of made up his mind...</i>

386
00:32:35.587 --> 00:32:39.824
and he said he was going with John
Cantley. who's another colleague.

387
00:32:39.825 --> 00:32:44.862
a British photo journalist that we'd
all met in Libya the year before.

388
00:32:44.863 --> 00:32:47.372
- <i>What's your name?</i>
- Jim.

389
00:32:47.666 --> 00:32:49.967
- John.
- Jim and John.

390
00:32:50.468 --> 00:32:54.405
<i>Johnny. you say I love you. you say.</i>

391
00:32:54.406 --> 00:32:58.843
I don't know. I... I really... didn't really...

392
00:32:58.844 --> 00:33:02.580
get into it with Jim. I think
I made it too easy for him.

393
00:33:02.581 --> 00:33:06.951
<i>I mean... you know. it was something
he wanted to do and so...</i>

394
00:33:06.952 --> 00:33:12.557
we were trying to be supportive about
his decision to do that. you know?

395
00:33:12.558 --> 00:33:18.328
You just want to punch him in the face.
You know. in a loving. brotherly way.

396
00:33:18.329 --> 00:33:22.373
you know. but you're like.
"Come on. Jim! Come on!"

397
00:33:23.469 --> 00:33:26.704
<i><font color=#FF551>TOM:</font> The last conversation I ever
had with Jim. I said to him. like.</i>

398
00:33:26.705 --> 00:33:29.573
"Jim. my man. why do you keep going
back into Syria? Like. I mean.

399
00:33:29.574 --> 00:33:32.142
what's it like?" He's like "It's
crazy. its crazy." I'm like.

400
00:33:32.143 --> 00:33:34.612
"Well. is it more dangerous than Libya?"

401
00:33:34.613 --> 00:33:38.889
He's like. "Yeah. it's more dangerous than
Libya!" I'm like. "You got captured in Libya!"

402
00:33:39.751 --> 00:33:42.660
You remember. you couldn't talk him out of it.

403
00:33:43.955 --> 00:33:47.365
<font color=#ff00ff>[Local Music Playing On Radio]</font>

404
00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:24.962
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> You know the thing is... is
there's physical courage. right?</i>

405
00:34:24.963 --> 00:34:27.297
<i>For some reason I have physical courage.</i>

406
00:34:27.298 --> 00:34:31.335
<i>But really. think about it. that's nothing
compared to moral courage.</i>

407
00:34:31.336 --> 00:34:36.974
<i>I can go and get those shots. but if I don't
have the moral courage to challenge authority.</i>

408
00:34:36.975 --> 00:34:41.746
<i>to write about things that are gonna
maybe have reprisals on my career.</i>

409
00:34:41.747 --> 00:34:44.922
<i>If I don't have that moral courage.
we don't have journalism.</i>

410
00:34:46.351 --> 00:34:49.520
Jim chose one story in particular
about this hospital.

411
00:34:49.521 --> 00:34:52.457
<i>The Dar Al Shifaa hospital in Aleppo.</i>

412
00:34:52.458 --> 00:34:56.160
<i>It was actually Jim's idea to
spend a week in that hospital</i>

413
00:34:56.161 --> 00:35:01.539
<i>documenting what the doctors and the
staff there were doing on a daily basis.</i>

414
00:35:01.533 --> 00:35:03.308
<font color=#ff00ff>[Jim Speaking]</font>

415
00:35:20.986 --> 00:35:24.589
You know. from what he and I witnessed.
they were literally shuttling people with

416
00:35:24.590 --> 00:35:30.294
<i>drips and you know. bandages and everything
in these tiny little taxis and cars.</i>

417
00:35:30.295 --> 00:35:33.363
<i>And Jim was the one who came up
with the idea of raising money</i>

418
00:35:33.364 --> 00:35:35.533
<i>to get an ambulance for the hospital.</i>

419
00:35:35.534 --> 00:35:38.869
And he was in touch with everybody
and there was an ambulance

420
00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:40.904
that was like a secondhand ambulance that was

421
00:35:40.905 --> 00:35:44.942
<i>coming from Austria and Jim and I actually
made one trip in and we saw the ambulance</i>

422
00:35:44.943 --> 00:35:46.910
<i>sitting outside of the hospital.</i>

423
00:35:46.911 --> 00:35:51.849
And that moment of joy on his face
was priceless. He was just like.

424
00:35:51.850 --> 00:35:53.991
<i>"It made it. that's awesome."</i>

425
00:35:56.654 --> 00:35:59.890
<i><font color=#FF551>CLARE:</font> I would say the flip
side of his willingness</i>

426
00:35:59.891 --> 00:36:02.026
<i>to get out there and do things</i>

427
00:36:02.027 --> 00:36:05.663
is that like. okay. you get
an ambulance into Syria.

428
00:36:05.664 --> 00:36:09.399
some militia is gonna commandeer it and use
it for their own purposes. they're gonna

429
00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:13.037
put an anti aircraft in the back
of it. like go blow shit up.

430
00:36:13.038 --> 00:36:16.641
Jim was not focused on that kind of issue.

431
00:36:16.642 --> 00:36:20.945
Like he would think about
what's the best-case scenario.

432
00:36:20.946 --> 00:36:23.821
<i>not how things could go wrong.</i>

433
00:36:27.418 --> 00:36:29.187
<i><font color=#FF551>NICOLE:</font> It becomes very personal</i>

434
00:36:29.188 --> 00:36:32.389
<i>because we have to live amongst
the local population.</i>

435
00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:36.560
<i>and it's such like this brutal. endless conflict</i>

436
00:36:36.561 --> 00:36:42.633
that... you just. you feel so. kind
of... alone in the sense that

437
00:36:42.634 --> 00:36:46.470
<i>you can't do anything about it. you
can't do anything for these people.</i>

438
00:36:46.471 --> 00:36:49.373
<i>For the people you've made friends with.
and for the people who took you in</i>

439
00:36:49.374 --> 00:36:54.311
<i>and shared their food with you and
wanted you to play with their children.</i>

440
00:36:54.312 --> 00:36:57.014
We become the intimate
chroniclers of this conflict.

441
00:36:57.015 --> 00:36:59.083
We don't have bureaus to go back to.

442
00:36:59.084 --> 00:37:04.288
<i>You're there and every moment.
of it. you share with the locals.</i>

443
00:37:04.289 --> 00:37:08.492
And I think there was just this enormous
guilt that rode on Jim's back

444
00:37:08.493 --> 00:37:15.465
that made him feel so compelled to do much
more than just record video and file it.

445
00:37:15.466 --> 00:37:18.603
<i>There were times where he was
offering up video for free</i>

446
00:37:18.604 --> 00:37:21.071
and I would chastise him for it.
I'd be like. "What are you doing?"

447
00:37:21.072 --> 00:37:23.808
He was like "Nah. you know.
whatever. it's fine. it's all good."

448
00:37:23.809 --> 00:37:27.151
<i>and "I just want to make sure
the video gets out there."</i>

449
00:37:29.781 --> 00:37:33.684
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> Jim's the kind of guy
who never needed much.</i>

450
00:37:33.685 --> 00:37:38.022
Some of the lifestyle of a
conflict journalist is tough.

451
00:37:38.023 --> 00:37:40.390
<i>but that didn't bother Jim.</i>

452
00:37:40.391 --> 00:37:44.028
The only possessions I think Jim
cared about were books and CD’s.

453
00:37:44.029 --> 00:37:46.096
<i>I mean. his camera ultimately.</i>

454
00:37:46.097 --> 00:37:51.702
He'd come home without a toothbrush. just
use whatever toothbrush was available.

455
00:37:51.703 --> 00:37:57.441
Jim could fall asleep anywhere. All he
needed was just a little space on the floor.

456
00:37:57.442 --> 00:37:59.917
<i>He was like a cat.</i>

457
00:38:00.746 --> 00:38:03.688
This is a good one.

458
00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:17.361
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> Over time. you know. he slowly
got rid of his apartment. sold his car.</i>

459
00:38:17.362 --> 00:38:21.131
<i>He just ended up really owning nothing.</i>

460
00:38:21.132 --> 00:38:24.702
<i>So. what he would want for Christmas.
this last time before going to Syria.</i>

461
00:38:24.703 --> 00:38:26.671
<i>he wanted a tough pair of pants.</i>

462
00:38:26.672 --> 00:38:30.607
He really did have less and less
and it didn't bother him at all

463
00:38:30.608 --> 00:38:33.150
because he seemed richer and richer.

464
00:38:45.023 --> 00:38:48.325
<i><font color=#FF551>NICOLE:</font> There was one day in
August. one Syrian activist</i>

465
00:38:48.326 --> 00:38:53.263
<i>was taking us around this neighborhood
called Bustan Al Kasa.</i>

466
00:38:53.264 --> 00:38:58.835
<i>and this fighter jet just started circling
above and just swooped right down</i>

467
00:38:58.836 --> 00:39:03.347
<i>and hit a building that was a
couple hundred feet from us.</i>

468
00:39:03.842 --> 00:39:05.650
<font color=#ff00ff>[Explosions]</font>

469
00:39:07.813 --> 00:39:14.004
<i>We started seeing the civilians coming out...
and just clutching nothing really. just...</i>

470
00:39:14.005 --> 00:39:17.254
<i>you know. ashen faces. there
was rubble everywhere.</i>

471
00:39:17.255 --> 00:39:19.363
It was chaos.

472
00:39:22.828 --> 00:39:25.762
<i>And the plane came around
again. dropped another bomb</i>

473
00:39:25.763 --> 00:39:28.965
really close by and actually we
were right across the building

474
00:39:28.966 --> 00:39:33.510
<i>and we looked up and we can see
the rubble start coming down.</i>

475
00:39:37.708 --> 00:39:43.987
That bomb had hit a family of. uh... seven.

476
00:39:48.553 --> 00:39:49.927
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Who was killed?</i>

477
00:39:50.856 --> 00:39:52.563
<i><font color=#FF551>JIM:</font> Who?</i>

478
00:39:55.861 --> 00:39:59.263
<i><font color=#FF551>NICOLE:</font> It was horrific in the
scale of it. but also just...</i>

479
00:39:59.264 --> 00:40:04.769
I think nothing prepares you for seeing
kids being killed and maimed in that way.

480
00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:07.355
and I... and I know that Jim really loves kids. so.

481
00:40:07.356 --> 00:40:10.941
you know. we were both just...
we didn't say anything

482
00:40:10.942 --> 00:40:15.886
<i>until we got to the field hospital where
they were bringing the bodies.</i>

483
00:40:21.352 --> 00:40:27.291
<i>And we were both just in this mode of
just needing to get the pictures out.</i>

484
00:40:27.292 --> 00:40:28.933
I think when we were finished

485
00:40:28.934 --> 00:40:33.762
that night though. we kinda like sat
down. and lit a cigarette and...

486
00:40:33.763 --> 00:40:37.034
we just started talking about it and
really there wasn't very much to say

487
00:40:37.035 --> 00:40:39.202
though. you know. like.

488
00:40:39.203 --> 00:40:42.606
<i>what is there to talk about when
you witness something like that?</i>

489
00:40:42.607 --> 00:40:45.415
<i>So we just sort of sat in silence.</i>

490
00:40:46.912 --> 00:40:51.315
<i><font color=#FF551>ZAC:</font> Nothing prepares you for that.
like no amount of courses. nothing.</i>

491
00:40:51.316 --> 00:40:55.219
<i>You just go there and you'll
either handle it or you don't.</i>

492
00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:59.523
And that's cool. Like either
you run. or you stay.

493
00:40:59.524 --> 00:41:02.559
And there's... not one is better than the other.

494
00:41:02.560 --> 00:41:06.896
<i>but just don't delude yourself.
Some people aren't meant for that.</i>

495
00:41:06.897 --> 00:41:08.706
<font color=#ff00ff>[Screaming]</font>

496
00:41:17.508 --> 00:41:21.245
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> The shine was starting
to come off in a way.</i>

497
00:41:21.246 --> 00:41:25.983
There was a period of time where journalists
were welcomed with open arms

498
00:41:25.984 --> 00:41:28.718
<i>because they'd seen what had happened in Libya</i>

499
00:41:28.719 --> 00:41:34.424
<i>and when that didn't come about after a year.
after year and a half. after two years.</i>

500
00:41:34.425 --> 00:41:37.127
it's just like. okay. what are you
guys doing? You know I had a doctor

501
00:41:37.128 --> 00:41:41.365
tell me at the hospital that Jim helped
raise money for an ambulance.

502
00:41:41.366 --> 00:41:44.701
He was like. "Look. you guys are
in and out of here since one year.

503
00:41:44.702 --> 00:41:50.014
and it's the same exact thing except
it's worse. I don't wanna talk to you."

504
00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:56.947
<i>If the populace on the ground
whose side you're documenting</i>

505
00:41:56.948 --> 00:42:01.986
<i>is getting more uneasy with
you. or less willing to help.</i>

506
00:42:01.987 --> 00:42:07.397
you're very dependent on the goodwill
of the people you're around.

507
00:42:35.153 --> 00:42:40.324
It's just that moment of like. how
well can you know anybody?

508
00:42:40.325 --> 00:42:42.960
<i>Even say you know someone very well.</i>

509
00:42:42.961 --> 00:42:46.897
<i>Two years of war. three years of war.
that's going to change anybody.</i>

510
00:42:46.898 --> 00:42:50.467
They warned journalists. they were
like. Al Qaeda is coming. you know.

511
00:42:50.468 --> 00:42:52.035
maybe even worse than Al Qaeda is coming

512
00:42:52.036 --> 00:42:58.042
and nobody is going to help us against
the Assad regime except for these guys.

513
00:42:58.043 --> 00:43:00.618
<i>So it was all there.</i>

514
00:43:04.449 --> 00:43:08.552
<i>One of the main things I noticed
the last time when he came out.</i>

515
00:43:08.553 --> 00:43:12.422
he looked really hollow and
he was quite... quite silent.

516
00:43:12.423 --> 00:43:16.660
<i>You know. he had like that amazing
room-brightening smile</i>

517
00:43:16.661 --> 00:43:22.707
even if he had seen terrible things. as
one does. It was disheartening to see.

518
00:43:27.672 --> 00:43:31.241
<i><font color=#FF551>MARK:</font> Before he left for Syria. I think we made
it a point. we were gonna bring him down</i>

519
00:43:31.242 --> 00:43:36.246
to Nathaniel Hall district and go
to the comedy club down there.

520
00:43:36.247 --> 00:43:39.316
<i>The comedians were horrible. but
like I was in the mood to laugh.</i>

521
00:43:39.317 --> 00:43:41.385
so I'm just laughing at you know. anything.

522
00:43:41.386 --> 00:43:45.389
And I just remember looking at
Jim and he was just dead faced.

523
00:43:45.390 --> 00:43:50.460
<i>And then me and Jim went outside
for a cigarette. like we always do.</i>

524
00:43:50.461 --> 00:43:56.265
<i>he said he had to go. We had a long hug. I
remember I hugged him a really long time.</i>

525
00:43:56.266 --> 00:43:59.269
<i>Just like hugged him really
tight. I said be careful.</i>

526
00:43:59.270 --> 00:44:02.172
Obviously I didn't feel like that was
the last time I was gonna see him.

527
00:44:02.173 --> 00:44:07.016
but it was... it was a good night... a
good end of the night. you know?

528
00:44:09.314 --> 00:44:12.649
<i><font color=#FF551>JOHN SR.:</font> I guess If I had
any regrets. Brian. that I</i>

529
00:44:12.650 --> 00:44:16.620
regret that I found it difficult to
communicate with Jim. um...

530
00:44:16.621 --> 00:44:20.990
<i>I don't know if it's the male thing or
whatever it is. but I just wish I was able</i>

531
00:44:20.991 --> 00:44:25.195
<i>to share more of who I was with Jimmy and
get him to share who he was with me.</i>

532
00:44:25.196 --> 00:44:28.432
which might have been just
as difficult. you know?

533
00:44:28.433 --> 00:44:31.435
<i>He would interview us when he came home.</i>

534
00:44:31.436 --> 00:44:36.806
<i>and he did a great job interviewing us 'cause
you felt like talking because he was listening.</i>

535
00:44:36.807 --> 00:44:39.008
You know. he wanted to know how we were.

536
00:44:39.009 --> 00:44:41.745
And that was when he came home.
that's what he wanted to know.

537
00:44:41.746 --> 00:44:45.115
He wanted. "Well. how are you. how you doing?"

538
00:44:45.116 --> 00:44:48.418
You know. "How's Grandma. how's Katie and Mark?"

539
00:44:48.419 --> 00:44:51.788
<i>And he just wanted to know how
everyone was doing. you know?</i>

540
00:44:51.789 --> 00:44:55.766
<i>So in that way. Jim was kind of solitary.</i>

541
00:44:57.628 --> 00:45:03.067
He was home. end of October. right before
uh... he went back for Syria that last time

542
00:45:03.068 --> 00:45:06.103
and. uh... he was going... I
remember he was leaving here

543
00:45:06.104 --> 00:45:08.705
and he was going to New York to get a helmet

544
00:45:08.706 --> 00:45:13.410
from somebody which was good. we were like.
"Getting safety equipment. we like this!"

545
00:45:13.411 --> 00:45:17.081
Yeah. I remember we left and we
dropped him off at a train station

546
00:45:17.082 --> 00:45:19.249
and he. you know. we were gonna
see him again in December.

547
00:45:19.250 --> 00:45:22.252
- He was supposed to come home kind of...
- Oh. yeah. you have a good memory. yeah.

548
00:45:22.253 --> 00:45:27.657
He was supposed to come home mid December.
And. uh... we were like. be safe. see you soon.

549
00:45:27.658 --> 00:45:33.103
- and unfortunately. that didn't...
- Yeah.

550
00:45:37.868 --> 00:45:42.412
<font color=#ff00ff>- [Sobbing]</font> Sorry.
- It's okay. I think that's enough.

551
00:45:55.886 --> 00:46:00.324
<i><font color=#FF551>NICOLE:</font> We'd spent the beginning of
November in Aleppo again with Jim.</i>

552
00:46:00.325 --> 00:46:05.362
<i>John Cantley and Mustafa. our translator
who's become a friend of ours.</i>

553
00:46:05.363 --> 00:46:09.199
I had had some issues with
my camera that week.

554
00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:14.845
so. I just was like. "Jim. I gotta go back
to Istanbul. I'll see you guys in a week".

555
00:46:16.374 --> 00:46:19.876
You know the moment when I said bye to Jim.

556
00:46:19.877 --> 00:46:23.847
<i>I had this feeling of reluctance to leave.</i>

557
00:46:23.848 --> 00:46:30.320
<i>I think in a way maybe it did upset the
balance that he and I had shared for so long.</i>

558
00:46:30.321 --> 00:46:33.730
<i>There are superstitions
when you're in a war zone.</i>

559
00:46:36.394 --> 00:46:40.897
There's like this one thing he and I
shared which was our lucky lighter.

560
00:46:40.898 --> 00:46:45.502
<i>It's very common in the middle east. It's like
the evil eye to ward off evil spirits. you know?</i>

561
00:46:45.503 --> 00:46:49.506
<i>We'd used it for everything and for some
reason it never ran out of lighter fluid.</i>

562
00:46:49.507 --> 00:46:53.943
It's just like this stupid idea. you
put your hopes into one object

563
00:46:53.944 --> 00:46:57.714
<i>to make it feel safe.</i>

564
00:46:57.715 --> 00:47:04.288
<i>I think about it a lot afterwards
that he didn't have it with him.</i>

565
00:47:04.289 --> 00:47:09.458
Maybe if I just gave him the lucky lighter.
everything would have turned out okay?

566
00:47:09.459 --> 00:47:10.767
I don't know.

567
00:47:12.930 --> 00:47:17.534
<i>That day. I was in Reyhanli.
which is the border town.</i>

568
00:47:17.535 --> 00:47:20.804
<i>and I would have seen them in about 5:00.</i>

569
00:47:20.805 --> 00:47:23.507
So I checked in and I told Jim. I'm like.

570
00:47:23.508 --> 00:47:28.918
"Hey. you know I'm here. um...
so text me when you get in."

571
00:47:29.647 --> 00:47:33.283
<i>You know. 5:00 rolls by and I started to worry.</i>

572
00:47:33.284 --> 00:47:37.386
<i>7:00. 8:00 rolls around and I'm like.
"Something is really wrong".</i>

573
00:47:37.387 --> 00:47:44.828
So I called Mustafa. and the first thing
he said to me was "Nicole. I'm so sorry.

574
00:47:44.829 --> 00:47:49.533
Um. I didn't... I couldn't do anything." I
was like. "What are you talking about.

575
00:47:49.534 --> 00:47:51.268
<i>what happened?"</i>

576
00:47:51.269 --> 00:47:54.571
<i>He was like. "You know. we were coming. We
were in the taxi. we were coming to Turkey</i>

577
00:47:54.572 --> 00:47:59.643
<i>"to meet you and this van with
these four guys with guns.</i>

578
00:47:59.644 --> 00:48:02.746
<i>"they stopped us on the road and they told us</i>

579
00:48:02.747 --> 00:48:06.883
<i>"to get out and they were pointing
their guns at us and screaming</i>

580
00:48:06.884 --> 00:48:10.920
<i>"and the gunman made Mustafa tie up their hands</i>

581
00:48:10.921 --> 00:48:15.299
<i>"and they put John and Jim
into the back of their van."</i>

582
00:48:16.827 --> 00:48:23.007
So I hung up and I just started
crying. I was like. um...

583
00:48:26.437 --> 00:48:31.615
I didn't know if I was gonna see Jim again and
that was the first thought that came to my head.

584
00:48:36.914 --> 00:48:41.050
It was like almost surreal... just.
this is a bad dream. It's not...

585
00:48:41.051 --> 00:48:43.887
It's never really happening. you
know. it's not happening.

586
00:48:43.888 --> 00:48:49.132
This can't be. you know. this can't happen
again... we can't do this again.

587
00:48:52.630 --> 00:48:56.962
Yeah. it was... you know. and
I dove in just like before.

588
00:48:56.963 --> 00:49:02.372
<i>I felt like. okay. it's going to be 45 to 100
days of hell and then we'll have him back.</i>

589
00:49:02.373 --> 00:49:05.475
<i>In a matter of a week. you could
tell it was very different.</i>

590
00:49:05.476 --> 00:49:08.412
A lot of misinformation as
opposed to last time in Libya.

591
00:49:08.413 --> 00:49:11.481
after a week went by. we knew where he was.

592
00:49:11.482 --> 00:49:15.618
who to deal with. so we just concentrated
on routes to get to one person.

593
00:49:15.619 --> 00:49:18.522
<i>Here was a mystery right out of a
crime show or something. right?</i>

594
00:49:18.523 --> 00:49:21.991
<i>Where you're trying to piece
together bits of information.</i>

595
00:49:21.992 --> 00:49:26.796
For the next three weeks. there was
just dead ends and false information

596
00:49:26.797 --> 00:49:30.667
and rumors and people being
scared of talking because

597
00:49:30.668 --> 00:49:34.070
<i>they had a suspicion of who
maybe was responsible.</i>

598
00:49:34.071 --> 00:49:36.472
<i>and they didn't want to get entangled in it.</i>

599
00:49:36.473 --> 00:49:42.445
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> Phil Balboni offered to stand
up a security team to try to find Jim.</i>

600
00:49:42.446 --> 00:49:46.716
So all these people were trying
so hard to get in place.

601
00:49:46.717 --> 00:49:50.794
but it was a very. very chaotic. confusing time.

602
00:49:52.890 --> 00:49:56.460
<i><font color=#FF551>JOHN JR.:</font> You're on eggshells.
you're just waiting to hear.</i>

603
00:49:56.461 --> 00:49:59.629
It's exhausting and I know
Jim felt guilty for that

604
00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:02.999
and I'm not trying to make him feel more guilty.

605
00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:09.406
but it's just... it's just a toll
that's taken by the families.

606
00:50:09.407 --> 00:50:13.477
<i><font color=#FF551>BRIAN:</font> What were the hurdles getting
White House and FBI involved?</i>

607
00:50:13.478 --> 00:50:17.547
<i>It's very tough to get action. and I understand
that. you know. the world is a big place.</i>

608
00:50:17.548 --> 00:50:23.487
<i>and so I actually felt guilty sometimes
trying to not to ask too much of them.</i>

609
00:50:23.488 --> 00:50:26.455
You know. Jim made this decision.
but. you know. just...

610
00:50:26.456 --> 00:50:29.358
just give it your best attention
and... and... and we'll trust you.

611
00:50:29.359 --> 00:50:32.161
That's kind of where it started. the relationship.

612
00:50:32.162 --> 00:50:36.500
And then I met the first agent that came over

613
00:50:36.501 --> 00:50:41.137
and. uh... it was just a kid out of school
and his first question to me was.

614
00:50:41.138 --> 00:50:43.773
<i>had I asked the regime for assistance?</i>

615
00:50:43.774 --> 00:50:48.144
Are you fucking kidding me? I called the
regime and asked them for assistance?

616
00:50:48.145 --> 00:50:52.048
Uh. no. I hadn't thought of that. thank
you. Thank you very much for that tip.

617
00:50:52.049 --> 00:50:55.919
They told us... they advised us to be quiet.

618
00:50:55.920 --> 00:51:00.823
because hopefully. you know. they could
find him and get him out and such.

619
00:51:00.824 --> 00:51:04.428
So we didn't say anything. so we went
through Christmas and all that.

620
00:51:04.429 --> 00:51:09.105
<i>you know. not telling anyone. but our
closest family that Jim was missing.</i>

621
00:51:10.367 --> 00:51:15.639
In some ways. it was better because
I didn't have to explain it.

622
00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:19.442
Because at times. it could
feel like accusatory. like.

623
00:51:19.443 --> 00:51:24.113
"Well. he was in Syria". That's not fair. like.

624
00:51:24.114 --> 00:51:27.951
you don't do that with police or firemen or
something like that. who do dangerous jobs.

625
00:51:27.952 --> 00:51:30.820
You don't say. "Well. you were in a fire.
what did you think would happen?"

626
00:51:30.821 --> 00:51:35.164
My friend doesn't need to
explain why he's a journalist.

627
00:51:36.060 --> 00:51:41.631
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> Come the new year. I
couldn't stand it. I was frantic.</i>

628
00:51:41.632 --> 00:51:44.734
So we chose to go public.

629
00:51:44.735 --> 00:51:47.670
<i><font color=#FF551>JOHN SR.:</font> I appeal to the people who have Jim.</i>

630
00:51:47.671 --> 00:51:51.475
to give us some information in
terms of his welfare. his health?

631
00:51:51.476 --> 00:51:56.680
It breaks my heart that the
persons who have captured him

632
00:51:56.681 --> 00:51:59.949
don't understand his goodness.

633
00:51:59.950 --> 00:52:04.153
My personal feeling is that
silence helps two people.

634
00:52:04.154 --> 00:52:08.658
One is the government. it doesn't
push them to do more sooner.

635
00:52:08.659 --> 00:52:12.896
and the captors. it allows them
to do whatever they want.

636
00:52:12.897 --> 00:52:18.234
It's difficult now with all the
talk about the Islamic State.

637
00:52:18.235 --> 00:52:21.571
<i>they've become so famous or infamous.</i>

638
00:52:21.572 --> 00:52:24.641
<i>but the Islamic State. ISIS/ISIL</i>

639
00:52:24.642 --> 00:52:28.084
<i>was on nobody's radar at the time.</i>

640
00:52:28.646 --> 00:52:34.183
With a very high degree of confidence.
we now believe that Jim was abducted

641
00:52:34.184 --> 00:52:37.653
by a pro-government militia
group and was subsequently

642
00:52:37.654 --> 00:52:39.723
turned over to Syrian government forces.

643
00:52:39.724 --> 00:52:43.627
This is the first time we've really
heard anything like this. so.

644
00:52:43.628 --> 00:52:46.696
we are very hopeful like John says. you know?

645
00:52:46.697 --> 00:52:50.506
Well. it turned out we were
dead wrong about that.

646
00:52:51.636 --> 00:52:54.503
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> All the information
upfront was just a bunch of BS.</i>

647
00:52:54.504 --> 00:52:58.542
<i>No one knew what the heck they were
talking about because we had no access.</i>

648
00:52:58.543 --> 00:53:04.714
That whole year. I don't care what anyone says.
all the efforts. all the leads. all this and that.

649
00:53:04.715 --> 00:53:07.431
we were in the wrong area of the country.

650
00:53:07.432 --> 00:53:10.987
<i>You know. and Syria is about the size of
New England. maybe. a little smaller.</i>

651
00:53:10.988 --> 00:53:15.231
So here you go. Brian. go
into New England. find Jim.

652
00:53:16.160 --> 00:53:18.004
I think the moment I learned
that he had been kidnapped.

653
00:53:18.005 --> 00:53:21.264
I was just like this is going
to be a really long process.

654
00:53:21.265 --> 00:53:26.736
Um. but I'm gonna do anything that
I can in my power to get him home.

655
00:53:26.737 --> 00:53:33.376
Because. um... because I can't stand the
thought of him being in a cell somewhere.

656
00:53:33.377 --> 00:53:38.922
<i>cold and hungry. and I can't sit
here and not try to look for him.</i>

657
00:53:55.766 --> 00:54:00.444
<i>My name is Daniel and I'm
a Danish photojournalist.</i>

658
00:54:00.905 --> 00:54:04.207
<i>I started as a... a gymnast.</i>

659
00:54:04.208 --> 00:54:09.512
<i>and while I spent all my time in gyms all
around the world doing gymnastics. I...</i>

660
00:54:09.513 --> 00:54:14.991
got bored when I didn't do anything
else and I started to take pictures.

661
00:54:17.321 --> 00:54:22.726
<i>I only planned to be inside Syria for two days.</i>

662
00:54:22.727 --> 00:54:30.473
I had one day of work when I walked
around this small. quiet town.

663
00:54:30.901 --> 00:54:33.537
<i>It was spring.</i>

664
00:54:33.538 --> 00:54:40.509
<i>So the weather started to be very better
and people seemed happy and relaxed.</i>

665
00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:47.590
<i>And we were told to go and... and...
and speak to some guys in the area.</i>

666
00:54:48.919 --> 00:54:53.823
<i>Very calmly. we're sitting in
sofas. they were offering tea.</i>

667
00:54:53.824 --> 00:54:58.662
Everything was calm and quiet. Even
though I knew that something was...

668
00:54:58.663 --> 00:55:00.864
strange. something was wrong.

669
00:55:00.865 --> 00:55:04.133
Uh... and then they just asked me to stand up.

670
00:55:04.134 --> 00:55:10.747
<i>and they took off my glasses. and they said.
"Don't worry" and "This is just a procedure".</i>

671
00:55:12.877 --> 00:55:16.412
That was how everything began
so. you know. a quiet Sunday.

672
00:55:16.413 --> 00:55:20.990
beautiful spring morning
became a nightmare for me.

673
00:55:28.726 --> 00:55:34.964
The longer you are a hostage. the easier it
becomes in some way. the better you get at it.

674
00:55:34.965 --> 00:55:41.871
<i>And I had like one and half month by myself.
and after I believe. two and a half month.</i>

675
00:55:41.872 --> 00:55:48.344
<i>I was put together with two other Westerners.
and then we were put together four.</i>

676
00:55:48.345 --> 00:55:53.917
<i>I think we were five together. and then 7
together. then came another one. eight.</i>

677
00:55:53.918 --> 00:55:57.026
<i>Then it just started to evolve.</i>

678
00:56:07.331 --> 00:56:11.534
<i>We were in that cell. British. Americans...</i>

679
00:56:11.535 --> 00:56:17.440
<i>French. Italian. German. Danish.
Russian. Spanish.</i>

680
00:56:17.441 --> 00:56:20.049
<i>We were 19 at one stage.</i>

681
00:58:00.644 --> 00:58:08.283
And one day we had to sit faces to the
wall. so. but I could see under my arms.

682
00:58:08.284 --> 00:58:12.721
<i>I could see. uh... some mattress were
moved in and there came some guys in</i>

683
00:58:12.722 --> 00:58:15.258
<i>traditional Syrian clothes</i>

684
00:58:15.259 --> 00:58:19.995
and then they closed the door again.
the big metal door. and I looked up

685
00:58:19.996 --> 00:58:23.732
<i>and there was James and John Cantley.</i>

686
00:58:23.733 --> 00:58:29.012
Everybody was like "Yay. welcome.
welcome!" You know. it's two new friends.

687
00:58:38.348 --> 00:58:43.052
You know. I created this... this picture
in my head of this big. like.

688
00:58:43.053 --> 00:58:47.456
war journalist and so I could
only get disappointed in a way.

689
00:58:47.457 --> 00:58:53.963
but... but. he... he... I remember him being
like. "Uh. what's happening?" Yeah and...

690
00:58:53.964 --> 00:58:57.773
So that was-basically the first time I met him.

691
00:58:58.268 --> 00:59:03.006
<i>But it was real different to be put
together with James and John</i>

692
00:59:03.007 --> 00:59:07.510
because they've been together in prison
for almost a year. When I first saw them.

693
00:59:07.511 --> 00:59:12.548
<i>they was the most experienced
of us and. uh... I started</i>

694
00:59:12.549 --> 00:59:18.527
from the beginning and I think the whole group
started to... to lean a little bit against them.

695
01:00:04.601 --> 01:00:08.204
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> James was very silent most of the time.</i>

696
01:00:08.205 --> 01:00:11.107
He was very good at listening.

697
01:00:11.108 --> 01:00:16.144
<i>He managed to make the room bigger
in a way by being small himself</i>

698
01:00:16.145 --> 01:00:19.048
<i>and that is a very. very difficult thing to be.</i>

699
01:00:19.049 --> 01:00:24.193
And you really want to scream in the
head of everybody like. "Fuck off!"

700
01:00:31.528 --> 01:00:33.870
<font color=#ff00ff>[Nicolas Speaking]</font>

701
01:01:40.297 --> 01:01:45.501
I remember one time James was
asked to stand up the whole night

702
01:01:45.502 --> 01:01:47.977
in the middle of the room.

703
01:02:58.708 --> 01:03:01.777
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> Late at night. there was no light at all.</i>

704
01:03:01.778 --> 01:03:05.448
<i>so we were just sitting there
in completely darkness</i>

705
01:03:05.449 --> 01:03:10.118
<i>and that time really. really. really went slowly.</i>

706
01:03:10.119 --> 01:03:14.457
<i>What we did. James and I. we
started to develop a way of</i>

707
01:03:14.458 --> 01:03:17.960
<i>passing through these hours of darkness</i>

708
01:03:17.961 --> 01:03:22.365
<i>by giving each other. like. massages.</i>

709
01:03:22.366 --> 01:03:30.946
And it sounds a maybe a little bit. you know.
uh... strange or gay. or whatever. but...

710
01:03:32.542 --> 01:03:37.312
But there was something. there
was something nice about it.

711
01:03:37.313 --> 01:03:41.250
And James asked me. "Can you teach me
how to give like a real nice massage.

712
01:03:41.251 --> 01:03:47.155
so when I get out. and I meet a woman.
I can... I can really impress her?"

713
01:03:47.156 --> 01:03:51.794
<i>So we started having these
kind of lessons. you know?</i>

714
01:03:51.795 --> 01:03:55.597
<i>Our body had witnessed a lot of trauma and</i>

715
01:03:55.598 --> 01:04:00.569
<i>the fact that somebody is actually touching
you and... and it's a nice feeling.</i>

716
01:04:00.570 --> 01:04:05.341
<i>for me. it was a nice way to
feel a little bit human again.</i>

717
01:04:05.342 --> 01:04:08.477
And James. he never learned
how to give a proper massage.

718
01:04:08.478 --> 01:04:11.680
it was awful every time. so he really.

719
01:04:11.681 --> 01:04:15.958
he really managed. to... to get
a good deal out of that one.

720
01:05:14.778 --> 01:05:19.315
<i>I remember one time. we were
given a lot of dates to eat</i>

721
01:05:19.316 --> 01:05:23.352
and at some point we were
moved and you just don't

722
01:05:23.353 --> 01:05:27.089
leave food behind. or destroy it or whatever.
but sometimes you have to do it

723
01:05:27.090 --> 01:05:29.925
because there's no where to put it.

724
01:05:29.926 --> 01:05:32.969
<font color=#ff00ff>[Nicolas Speaking]</font>

725
01:05:49.313 --> 01:05:54.049
James. he just took out his pants and
he took out like two-kilos of dates.

726
01:05:54.050 --> 01:05:56.192
<i>"Don't worry. guys."</i>

727
01:05:58.455 --> 01:06:01.690
He could have taken all the
food by himself later that night

728
01:06:01.691 --> 01:06:05.360
or whatever. but he always took the
things so he could share it around

729
01:06:05.361 --> 01:06:07.970
or give it to the people who didn't have it.

730
01:07:23.539 --> 01:07:28.310
<i>In the beginning of James' and John's
captivity. they were really starved.</i>

731
01:07:28.311 --> 01:07:32.747
They didn't like to talk about it. they didn't
find it very interesting to talk about.

732
01:07:32.748 --> 01:07:36.392
but one thing I know was that... uh...

733
01:07:38.422 --> 01:07:42.264
That they really. really.
really had a difficult time.

734
01:07:42.992 --> 01:07:49.237
<i>But they managed to get back on
track to gain strength again.</i>

735
01:07:50.333 --> 01:07:54.837
<i>It was very interesting to see what
happened between James and John</i>

736
01:07:54.838 --> 01:07:57.672
because they've been together
for almost a year.

737
01:07:57.673 --> 01:08:00.943
When I first saw them. that
meant they have spoken about

738
01:08:00.944 --> 01:08:04.046
every single thing there is to talk about.

739
01:08:04.047 --> 01:08:10.625
So I was basically the one starting
to listen to all James' story again.

740
01:08:14.423 --> 01:08:17.159
<i>There was a period of time
in the prison where we</i>

741
01:08:17.160 --> 01:08:20.729
<i>was not interrupted by the guards very often.</i>

742
01:08:20.730 --> 01:08:25.768
<i>It had meant that we could get a routine.
so we worked out. we did a trivia.</i>

743
01:08:25.769 --> 01:08:28.503
<i>we had lectures and stuff like this.</i>

744
01:08:28.504 --> 01:08:30.738
We managed after. I don't know.

745
01:08:30.739 --> 01:08:35.243
three weeks. one month or
so. to make this Risk game.

746
01:08:35.244 --> 01:08:38.287
<font color=#ff00ff>[Nicolas Speaking]</font>

747
01:08:56.065 --> 01:08:59.535
We had a small bucket that
we received some yogurt in

748
01:08:59.536 --> 01:09:04.706
and we cut out a piece of cardboard
and we made three lines

749
01:09:04.707 --> 01:09:09.744
and we put it in the bottom of the bucket and
then you should hold up a date seed and...

750
01:09:09.745 --> 01:09:11.479
and let it go.

751
01:09:11.480 --> 01:09:14.583
<i>and it would fall down and
whatever number it landed on.</i>

752
01:09:14.584 --> 01:09:18.960
it would be that number. So
that was our dice for the game.

753
01:09:30.233 --> 01:09:35.137
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> You know. take like ten journalists.
war. and put them into one room</i>

754
01:09:35.138 --> 01:09:37.139
<i>and make them play the game</i>

755
01:09:37.140 --> 01:09:40.743
<i>about taking control over the world. you know.</i>

756
01:09:40.744 --> 01:09:43.752
it's basically like putting
gasoline to a bonfire.

757
01:10:18.314 --> 01:10:22.650
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> We started to have our own small world</i>

758
01:10:22.651 --> 01:10:29.531
<i>that made everything much easier to survive
in a way. It was much easier to understand.</i>

759
01:10:30.759 --> 01:10:36.732
<i>We didn't have to think about economy. we
didn't have to think about bank loans or...</i>

760
01:10:36.733 --> 01:10:40.175
<i>the prices of gas at the moment.</i>

761
01:10:41.270 --> 01:10:44.006
<i>You adapt into the situation</i>

762
01:10:44.007 --> 01:10:48.743
<i>and then suddenly this... this whole
thing becomes a part of your life.</i>

763
01:10:48.744 --> 01:10:50.979
<i>This is your life.</i>

764
01:10:50.980 --> 01:10:55.717
When you look back on it. that's what
I remember was our small society.

765
01:10:55.718 --> 01:10:58.220
where we really start to know each other.

766
01:10:58.221 --> 01:11:03.532
You know who made this fart. you
could smell. this is the fart of you.

767
01:11:04.227 --> 01:11:06.269
<font color=#ff00ff>[Nicolas Speaking]</font>

768
01:11:25.681 --> 01:11:28.817
<i>I remember James' 40th birthday.</i>

769
01:11:28.818 --> 01:11:34.690
it was late at night. it was completely
dark. James said. "Oh.

770
01:11:34.691 --> 01:11:38.393
by the way. I. uh... I turn 40 today."

771
01:11:38.394 --> 01:11:43.531
I was just like. "What?" So we sang a song
for him and. uh... I remember that we said.

772
01:11:43.532 --> 01:11:47.675
<i>"We hope it would be a much
better birthday next year."</i>

773
01:12:25.709 --> 01:12:28.744
<i>Right here. One. two and three.</i>

774
01:12:28.745 --> 01:12:32.414
<i>Come on! Smile! Please! Nice! Good!</i>

775
01:12:32.415 --> 01:12:37.852
<i>We've been through a lot together.
Michael has co-signed loans for me.</i>

776
01:12:38.253 --> 01:12:41.156
He has lent me his professional clothes.

777
01:12:41.157 --> 01:12:44.526
his car. his dental plan.

778
01:12:44.527 --> 01:12:46.061
<font color=#ff00ff>[People Laughing]</font>

779
01:12:46.062 --> 01:12:49.064
And I think sometimes we struggle
to understand each other

780
01:12:49.065 --> 01:12:53.501
and where exactly we're coming from
and... why we do the things we do. uh.

781
01:12:53.502 --> 01:12:56.411
Michael has entirely too much common sense...

782
01:12:57.473 --> 01:13:00.843
and sometimes I have entirely
too little common sense.

783
01:13:00.844 --> 01:13:05.047
We've somehow grown closer
despite the differences

784
01:13:05.048 --> 01:13:09.658
<i>and it's. um... I guess it's about being brothers.</i>

785
01:13:11.788 --> 01:13:17.292
<i>I'd say the first 100. 150 days I was
all in. but I definitely retracted</i>

786
01:13:17.293 --> 01:13:22.930
pretty strongly after that. I have
a lot of regrets about not. uh.

787
01:13:22.931 --> 01:13:25.262
not continuing full steam. and...

788
01:13:25.763 --> 01:13:29.036
I don't know if it was because
I was trying to protect myself.

789
01:13:29.037 --> 01:13:32.941
<i>or I was just trying to protect
my family. and that you know.</i>

790
01:13:32.942 --> 01:13:37.812
give the kids the attention I have. you
know. I could argue that that's what Jim

791
01:13:37.813 --> 01:13:41.116
would prefer. and I don't know
it was all kinds of. of ex...

792
01:13:41.117 --> 01:13:45.460
excuses or reasons but I... it's
something I do feel terrible about.

793
01:13:46.289 --> 01:13:49.124
<i>But then. uh. then I got pulled
right back into it very strongly.</i>

794
01:13:49.125 --> 01:13:53.228
and very immediately when um...
the first e-mail came to me.

795
01:13:53.229 --> 01:14:00.168
"Hello. we have James and want to negotiate
for him. He is safe. He is our friend.

796
01:14:00.169 --> 01:14:04.706
"and we do not want to hurt him. If
you want cooperation we have rules.

797
01:14:04.707 --> 01:14:07.542
<i>"You cannot go to the media ever about this.</i>

798
01:14:07.543 --> 01:14:12.079
<i>"If you do. we will not negotiate.
We want money fast."</i>

799
01:14:12.080 --> 01:14:14.917
We shared everything we had with
everybody. you know. FBI knew.

800
01:14:14.918 --> 01:14:16.884
security team knew. everyone knew.

801
01:14:16.885 --> 01:14:19.086
They said. "Just keep them
talking" and all that.

802
01:14:19.087 --> 01:14:20.923
"they're just beginning their negotiations.

803
01:14:20.924 --> 01:14:23.258
we've got time. Just keep at it."

804
01:14:23.259 --> 01:14:27.094
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> So I. uh. after coordinating
with some officials replied to them.</i>

805
01:14:27.095 --> 01:14:31.599
"We've been concerned about Jim and want
to know that he's okay. Please provide us

806
01:14:31.600 --> 01:14:33.401
"with proof that you have Jim.

807
01:14:33.402 --> 01:14:36.371
<i>and we will be happy to
work things out with you."</i>

808
01:14:36.372 --> 01:14:38.706
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> We still didn't know
who was holding him.</i>

809
01:14:38.707 --> 01:14:41.909
It was obvious that they were
people against the Assad regime.

810
01:14:41.910 --> 01:14:47.149
but they didn't identify themselves any more
than that at all. They were very shrewd.

811
01:14:47.150 --> 01:14:51.619
And their e-mails unfortunately
were totally undetectable.

812
01:14:51.620 --> 01:14:54.622
About a week goes by. and they responded.

813
01:14:54.623 --> 01:14:57.392
<i>"James Wright Foley is being detained by us.</i>

814
01:14:57.393 --> 01:15:01.863
<i>"At this stage no video or picture evidence
of his well-being will be provided until</i>

815
01:15:01.864 --> 01:15:04.663
"we see tangible progress in
your efforts to negotiate.

816
01:15:04.664 --> 01:15:08.036
<i>"However you will be able to ask
three questions of a personal nature</i>

817
01:15:08.037 --> 01:15:10.805
<i>"that nobody except James
will know the answers to.</i>

818
01:15:10.806 --> 01:15:13.876
"And our primary demand is
that you use your influence to

819
01:15:13.877 --> 01:15:16.945
<i>"pressure your government to
release our Muslim prisoners.</i>

820
01:15:16.946 --> 01:15:20.983
<i>"who they have imprisoned.
whether innocent or 'guilty'</i>

821
01:15:20.984 --> 01:15:22.918
<i>"according to 'your laws.'</i>

822
01:15:22.919 --> 01:15:25.387
<i>"If this fails to bear any fruits.</i>

823
01:15:25.388 --> 01:15:30.325
<i>then our secondary demand is
the sum of 100 million Euros."</i>

824
01:15:30.326 --> 01:15:37.099
FBI. um. seemed to have their hands tied
because all they were able to do was

825
01:15:37.100 --> 01:15:40.134
okay our family e-mails.

826
01:15:40.135 --> 01:15:44.439
and they really weren't able to
help us much with strategy.

827
01:15:44.440 --> 01:15:47.309
<i>They just told us to be yourselves. be family.</i>

828
01:15:47.310 --> 01:15:51.045
<i>Tell them. "The truth is we
can't release any prisoners.</i>

829
01:15:51.046 --> 01:15:54.582
we certainly don't have a hundred million Euro."

830
01:15:54.583 --> 01:15:58.020
<i>Michael came up with some questions
I didn't know the answers to.</i>

831
01:15:58.021 --> 01:16:03.058
<i>but we sent those back to the captors.</i>

832
01:16:03.059 --> 01:16:05.527
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> Then there was some brief comment.</i>

833
01:16:05.528 --> 01:16:11.633
It says. uh... "James was detained
whilst operating as a 'journalist'.

834
01:16:11.634 --> 01:16:13.200
<i>"After his capture.</i>

835
01:16:13.201 --> 01:16:15.803
<i>"and the following interrogations
we came to learn that he had been</i>

836
01:16:15.804 --> 01:16:18.206
<i>"embedded with the US troops
serving in Afghanistan.</i>

837
01:16:18.207 --> 01:16:23.145
<i>"And that his brother. Michael Patrick Foley
is a serving officer in the US Air Force."</i>

838
01:16:23.146 --> 01:16:26.621
<i>But I was never in the Air Force. That was John.</i>

839
01:16:30.586 --> 01:16:37.392
My brother Jim... said that Mike
was the Air Force officer.

840
01:16:37.393 --> 01:16:40.177
It may have been a typo. it
may not have been. but...

841
01:16:40.178 --> 01:16:43.698
I believe it was my. you know. my big brother

842
01:16:43.699 --> 01:16:51.313
trying to protect me. You know.
you love your brothers. but...

843
01:16:56.279 --> 01:17:02.557
For him to put... be willing to put his
life on the line or his body on the line...

844
01:17:07.122 --> 01:17:10.532
for my protection is significant.

845
01:17:14.630 --> 01:17:17.039
<font color=#ff00ff>[Inaudible]</font>

846
01:17:47.296 --> 01:17:50.265
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> I think they came in
with the proof of life for John</i>

847
01:17:50.266 --> 01:17:52.800
<i>and then everybody else
got their proof of lives.</i>

848
01:17:52.801 --> 01:17:56.271
Everybody came in like. "Yes!"
and "We are happy". and...

849
01:17:56.272 --> 01:18:00.108
<i>but James didn't get his proof of life.</i>

850
01:18:00.109 --> 01:18:06.153
<i>Until one day that they... they came in
and they asked James to follow...</i>

851
01:18:06.915 --> 01:18:11.352
<i>and when he came back. you know. he came
back with his arms over his head and...</i>

852
01:18:11.353 --> 01:18:14.556
he said that this was the best day of his life.

853
01:18:14.557 --> 01:18:16.391
<i>And then him and John. they...</i>

854
01:18:16.392 --> 01:18:22.104
<i>they hugged each other and they were dancing
around like they just won the big lottery.</i>

855
01:18:24.100 --> 01:18:27.069
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> That was early December of 2013.</i>

856
01:18:27.070 --> 01:18:30.972
All the answers came back right
on. We knew they had Jim.

857
01:18:30.973 --> 01:18:32.640
<i>But by the end of December.</i>

858
01:18:32.641 --> 01:18:37.879
they e-mailed us back and said. "This
is the last e-mail you'll get from us".

859
01:18:37.880 --> 01:18:40.188
And cut off communication.

860
01:18:41.617 --> 01:18:45.420
<i><font color=#FF551>JOHN SR.:</font> I mean. the first year we just trusted
that the government would have this in hand</i>

861
01:18:45.421 --> 01:18:48.690
<i>and that. um. despite our lack
of information. etcetera.</i>

862
01:18:48.691 --> 01:18:51.093
they had been through this before.
they knew what to do.

863
01:18:51.094 --> 01:18:53.761
and you know. we were in good hands.

864
01:18:53.762 --> 01:18:57.532
At the end of that year we realized
that nothing was being done.

865
01:18:57.533 --> 01:19:02.744
<i>and that we were really going to
have to do something on our own.</i>

866
01:19:11.514 --> 01:19:15.316
<i><font color=#FF551>PHIL:</font> Diane was great about
meeting with ambassadors</i>

867
01:19:15.317 --> 01:19:21.989
<i>in Washington. from other countries
that might have some influence.</i>

868
01:19:21.990 --> 01:19:25.393
It turned out that no one had any influence.

869
01:19:25.394 --> 01:19:30.397
because the group that had him didn't listen
to anybody. But we didn't know that.

870
01:19:30.398 --> 01:19:33.568
<i><font color=#FF551>MALE REPORTER:</font> It's kind of come as a
surprise to a lot of us this group ISIS.</i>

871
01:19:33.569 --> 01:19:36.571
<i>A group that we hadn't really heard
much about. Who exactly are they?</i>

872
01:19:36.572 --> 01:19:38.906
<i><font color=#FF551>DR. SALLY LEIVESLEY:</font> Well. it's
a criminal marauding gang.</i>

873
01:19:38.907 --> 01:19:44.846
They come out of the original.
very brutal Iraqi terror group.

874
01:19:44.847 --> 01:19:48.250
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> They're the worst.
They're the worst of the worst.</i>

875
01:19:48.251 --> 01:19:50.218
<i>Washington doesn't know
how to deal with them.</i>

876
01:19:50.219 --> 01:19:53.608
How's this family in New England
gonna figure that out. right?

877
01:19:53.609 --> 01:19:56.658
<i>I mean. you're dealing with. um... pure evil.</i>

878
01:19:56.659 --> 01:20:01.203
<i>but a capable and organized group.</i>

879
01:20:55.784 --> 01:20:57.826
<font color=#ff00ff>[Pierre Speaking]</font>

880
01:21:15.304 --> 01:21:19.841
<i>They were very tough towards
me also. but no matter what.</i>

881
01:21:19.842 --> 01:21:25.079
they meant freedom for me. because
they were the one negotiating for me.

882
01:21:25.080 --> 01:21:31.192
But James and John were destroyed
by the Beatles in the beginning.

883
01:21:37.860 --> 01:21:42.730
<i><font color=#FF551>PIERRE:</font> He had this ability
to escape the situation.</i>

884
01:21:42.731 --> 01:21:49.376
<i>To. enjoy. you know. the sound
of children playing outside.</i>

885
01:21:52.341 --> 01:21:59.054
<i>Or enjoy the view of just some sun
entering through the window.</i>

886
01:22:05.588 --> 01:22:07.529
<font color=#ff00ff>[Didier Speaking]</font>

887
01:22:33.349 --> 01:22:38.025
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> James converted in
the beginning of his captivity.</i>

888
01:22:39.054 --> 01:22:47.262
And I know that was at the same period
as he was getting really bad treatment.

889
01:22:47.263 --> 01:22:53.267
It gave a good routine. Normally we have a
tradition of going into church every Sunday.

890
01:22:53.268 --> 01:22:55.169
But if you cannot do that.

891
01:22:55.170 --> 01:23:01.409
you need another way of feeling that
you are doing something with your faith.

892
01:23:01.410 --> 01:23:03.778
<i>And you can call it a surviving skill.</i>

893
01:23:03.779 --> 01:23:09.484
<i>You can call it just a way of being
interested in another culture.</i>

894
01:23:09.485 --> 01:23:15.729
What James used to say to me was
that. for him God is the same.

895
01:23:44.753 --> 01:23:47.555
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> We had such great Christmases
when the kids were little.</i>

896
01:23:47.556 --> 01:23:51.325
<i>Oh. just wonderful. wonderful Christmases and</i>

897
01:23:51.326 --> 01:23:55.897
you know as they got older
and Jim's siblings married.

898
01:23:55.898 --> 01:23:59.734
<i>they weren't always able to be
home. But Jim was always home.</i>

899
01:23:59.735 --> 01:24:04.671
Most of my memories of Jim
culminate around the holidays.

900
01:24:04.672 --> 01:24:09.210
<i>You know. the playing of the video games.
the board games. the Ping-Pong tournaments.</i>

901
01:24:09.211 --> 01:24:12.079
<i>the anything tournaments.</i>

902
01:24:12.080 --> 01:24:15.282
<font color=#ff00ff>- [Laughing]</font>
- <i>Grandma. Grandma. Grandma.</i>

903
01:24:15.283 --> 01:24:19.253
You wake up and you open the
gifts and you're like. "Ah. crap.

904
01:24:19.254 --> 01:24:23.598
Jim has my name." <font color=#ff00ff>[Laughing]</font>
"What's he gonna give me?"

905
01:24:24.993 --> 01:24:30.337
So. I shouldn't say that. It was good times.

906
01:24:31.233 --> 01:24:33.935
And then the last Christmas he was home.

907
01:24:33.936 --> 01:24:38.606
I remember him. like. rolling on
the floor with Michael's son Matty.

908
01:24:38.607 --> 01:24:41.148
<i>He loved that kid.</i>

909
01:24:42.510 --> 01:24:45.453
There's a million Christmas stories though.

910
01:24:50.686 --> 01:24:54.588
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> You know it's difficult for us
to celebrate Christmas in any way.</i>

911
01:24:54.589 --> 01:24:57.625
<i>and um. we didn't have any
presents to give each other.</i>

912
01:24:57.626 --> 01:25:03.497
So we... we decided to sit down in a circle.

913
01:25:03.498 --> 01:25:06.968
<i>then we had to say something
nice to each other.</i>

914
01:25:06.969 --> 01:25:11.906
<i>and I remember that I said to James that.
"James. the first time I met you was.</i>

915
01:25:11.907 --> 01:25:18.012
<i>"you know. in this prison and you looked
as confused as if you were just</i>

916
01:25:18.013 --> 01:25:20.782
<i>"dumped down on the earth
from the moon or something</i>

917
01:25:20.783 --> 01:25:26.020
"and you basically destroyed my whole
idea of this great war journalist.

918
01:25:26.021 --> 01:25:33.127
"James Wright Foley. and then suddenly
I find out that you are very clumsy.

919
01:25:33.128 --> 01:25:39.233
"you're very bad at sports.
but then... then again.

920
01:25:39.234 --> 01:25:44.772
you're... you're the most..." I think
I said to him. you know.

921
01:25:44.773 --> 01:25:52.013
"you're the most honest person. there
is no evil at all to find in you. James.

922
01:25:52.014 --> 01:25:55.416
"You are pure good. Sometimes too good.

923
01:25:55.417 --> 01:25:59.998
And I'm. I'm really happy that
I've. that I've met you."

924
01:26:03.691 --> 01:26:06.961
<i>That was our Christmas night.
When I went to bed that night</i>

925
01:26:06.962 --> 01:26:11.505
<i>I really felt that I had the best
Christmas night in my life.</i>

926
01:26:29.217 --> 01:26:31.593
<font color=#ff00ff>[Didier Speaking]</font>

927
01:26:41.296 --> 01:26:44.138
<font color=#ff00ff>[Pierre Speaking]</font>

928
01:28:02.044 --> 01:28:04.011
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> So that's when I
started to get more frantic.</i>

929
01:28:04.012 --> 01:28:10.133
I mean I thought. There's gotta be some
way to get the French and the US to talk.

930
01:28:10.134 --> 01:28:14.656
"Didier. this is my husband John."

931
01:28:14.657 --> 01:28:18.960
<i>They were willing to share all kinds of
things about Jim personally. how he was.</i>

932
01:28:18.961 --> 01:28:21.963
what they did. So I was
starting to get all this hope.

933
01:28:21.964 --> 01:28:25.332
and they have a hostage crisis unit in Paris.

934
01:28:25.333 --> 01:28:29.070
and they were very generous with their
time. So it was so different than what

935
01:28:29.071 --> 01:28:33.373
I was experiencing. so I was just
kind of like. "jeez." you know.

936
01:28:33.374 --> 01:28:38.585
"Jim and the others. our other Americans are
as important as these guys. aren't they?"

937
01:28:39.181 --> 01:28:46.453
<i>And it was the last night I was in Paris
and I got a phone call from John.</i>

938
01:28:46.454 --> 01:28:50.524
<i>He said. "Diane. we got another
email from the captors".</i>

939
01:28:50.525 --> 01:28:54.228
I thought. "Oh. great. what did they say?"

940
01:28:54.229 --> 01:28:58.139
<i>And that's when they threatened to kill Jim.</i>

941
01:29:01.670 --> 01:29:06.073
But me and my cluelessness.

942
01:29:06.074 --> 01:29:10.311
I was just excited that they reached out to us.

943
01:29:10.312 --> 01:29:13.748
<i>We had raised about a million dollars in pledges</i>

944
01:29:13.749 --> 01:29:16.150
and so I was so excited to hear
from them because we thought.

945
01:29:16.151 --> 01:29:21.795
now we can tell them. we have this
money. And I was just so clueless.

946
01:29:22.590 --> 01:29:26.193
<i>We knew that paying a ransom was illegal</i>

947
01:29:26.194 --> 01:29:31.232
and we also knew that. um...
it wouldn't have stopped us.

948
01:29:31.233 --> 01:29:34.701
<i>Foleys were prepared to mortgage their house</i>

949
01:29:34.702 --> 01:29:38.973
<i>and do what needed to be
done to make a payment.</i>

950
01:29:38.974 --> 01:29:45.780
I have a lot of evolving thoughts about this
whole process. and what the government

951
01:29:45.781 --> 01:29:51.052
didn't do that it should have done. I mean...

952
01:29:51.053 --> 01:29:57.058
if you just look at the facts. there are
15 European hostages who are alive

953
01:29:57.059 --> 01:30:01.703
<i>and with their families and
friends and loved ones today.</i>

954
01:30:04.166 --> 01:30:07.474
I wish we had started raising money sooner.

955
01:30:08.570 --> 01:30:14.015
I wish we had negotiated. I wish
it had turned out differently.

956
01:31:08.897 --> 01:31:11.999
<i>In some sense. I was okay with
the fact that he got captured.</i>

957
01:31:12.000 --> 01:31:15.937
'Cause I knew he was doing what he wanted to do.

958
01:31:15.938 --> 01:31:22.116
You know. and as a good family member and
as a good brother. I knew to understand that.

959
01:31:22.744 --> 01:31:25.913
<i>My mom did a great job in.
you know. keeping faith.</i>

960
01:31:25.914 --> 01:31:28.682
<i>and working as hard as she could. but...</i>

961
01:31:28.683 --> 01:31:33.560
from the get-go once it happened. I
kind of felt like he was already gone.

962
01:31:38.993 --> 01:31:43.264
<i><font color=#FF551>DANIEL:</font> So after a group of the other
hostages was released. the Beatles.</i>

963
01:31:43.265 --> 01:31:45.466
<i>they came back. the day after...</i>

964
01:31:45.467 --> 01:31:49.603
And they...

965
01:31:49.604 --> 01:31:54.248
They beated the shit out of James and I.

966
01:31:54.943 --> 01:31:56.944
<i>I think it's called a "Charlie horse"</i>

967
01:31:56.945 --> 01:32:02.383
<i>when you put your knee into
the legs of a person and...</i>

968
01:32:02.384 --> 01:32:08.089
<i>They did that to... to me and James while
we had to sit in a stretched position.</i>

969
01:32:08.090 --> 01:32:12.927
I don't know how many they gave
us. but. it just continued on us...

970
01:32:12.928 --> 01:32:17.404
never tried anything that hurt
so much in my entire life.

971
01:32:18.934 --> 01:32:23.604
<i>And then they... they just left.</i>

972
01:32:23.605 --> 01:32:29.175
<i>And I was just laying there crying. and I
couldn't be in my own body of pure pain</i>

973
01:32:29.176 --> 01:32:32.980
and James in the other corner of the room. he...

974
01:32:32.981 --> 01:32:38.953
I knew he got exactly the same treatment
as me. but I couldn't hear it.

975
01:32:38.954 --> 01:32:43.790
<i>He just. uh... I don't know if he
kept it inside. or how he did it.</i>

976
01:32:43.791 --> 01:32:47.829
<i>but only a few minutes after they
left James he kind of looked up</i>

977
01:32:47.830 --> 01:32:50.965
<i>and asked me if I was okay.</i>

978
01:32:50.966 --> 01:32:57.204
I was like "shut up. James". I remember I said.
"shut up. James." you know. you know.

979
01:32:57.205 --> 01:33:00.141
"Don't ask me if I'm okay." you know...

980
01:33:00.142 --> 01:33:04.485
"Don't worry about me." you
know. "worry about yourself".

981
01:33:05.347 --> 01:33:11.558
Yeah. we just laid there until the
pain started to disappear a bit.

982
01:33:12.420 --> 01:33:15.589
<i>Two or three days after they came in again.</i>

983
01:33:15.590 --> 01:33:21.328
This time. the Beatles just told us that.
"Now guys. everything's changed".

984
01:33:21.329 --> 01:33:27.397
<i>And they took all our food. our
games. most of the blankets.</i>

985
01:33:28.570 --> 01:33:32.139
<i>And they started to use. like police clubs.</i>

986
01:33:32.140 --> 01:33:36.578
<i>So every time we went to the toilet
we got beatings with a stick.</i>

987
01:33:37.079 --> 01:33:40.747
<i>We were so scared. we lost all hopes.</i>

988
01:33:40.748 --> 01:33:44.885
<i>No matter what came through
that door. it was evil.</i>

989
01:33:44.886 --> 01:33:51.032
Those 14 days before I got released
was the absolute worst times.

990
01:33:56.331 --> 01:34:01.468
<i>When people started to get released.
we decided to send out letters</i>

991
01:34:01.469 --> 01:34:04.771
<i>with the person who was released.</i>

992
01:34:04.772 --> 01:34:09.010
But James he didn't... he didn't
want to bother any of us.

993
01:34:09.011 --> 01:34:13.947
And I remember one day that.
I saw that John and James

994
01:34:13.948 --> 01:34:17.484
had been talking for some times in the corner.

995
01:34:17.485 --> 01:34:20.587
And after that John. he came
over to me and said "Daniel.

996
01:34:20.588 --> 01:34:22.089
<i>James he want to ask you something."</i>

997
01:34:22.090 --> 01:34:24.725
"Okay. okay. What up. James?" And he's like.

998
01:34:24.726 --> 01:34:28.695
"Oh. uh. it's just if. if. if you want to
carry out a message or something."

999
01:34:28.696 --> 01:34:32.499
You know. he was very. he said
it fa... he said it fast like.

1000
01:34:32.500 --> 01:34:35.309
like he didn't wanna bother me.

1001
01:34:36.471 --> 01:34:39.640
<i>There's many ways of dealing
with a situation like this</i>

1002
01:34:39.641 --> 01:34:44.378
<i>and James. I think one of the reasons
why he remained so strong.</i>

1003
01:34:44.379 --> 01:34:48.582
was because he managed to think
about all the good things.

1004
01:34:48.583 --> 01:34:52.353
<i>He saw the light instead of the dark spots.</i>

1005
01:34:52.354 --> 01:34:56.490
<i>where a guy like John. he
was much more realistic.</i>

1006
01:34:56.491 --> 01:35:00.134
<i>He knew when it was bad.</i>

1007
01:35:03.131 --> 01:35:08.402
<i>But I couldn't bring out the letter.
I was too afraid after these 14 days.</i>

1008
01:35:08.403 --> 01:35:12.139
<i>So I decided just to memorize
it as fast as I could.</i>

1009
01:35:12.140 --> 01:35:17.878
So I started waiting. I knew they will
come in the morning to pick me up.

1010
01:35:17.879 --> 01:35:21.382
<i>One day went. two days went.</i>

1011
01:35:21.383 --> 01:35:25.286
<i>and then fourth day. fifth day.</i>

1012
01:35:25.287 --> 01:35:29.356
Shit. man. And then I woke up
the sixth day in the morning.

1013
01:35:29.357 --> 01:35:31.725
and I couldn't sleep. I couldn't sleep all night.

1014
01:35:31.726 --> 01:35:36.730
<i>because I was so afraid of what
if they are playing a trick on me.</i>

1015
01:35:36.731 --> 01:35:41.935
<i>And James he walked over to me and he. he
sat down right next to me and. and he said.</i>

1016
01:35:41.936 --> 01:35:45.106
"Are you okay. Daniel?" and I said "Yeah. uh".

1017
01:35:45.107 --> 01:35:49.343
And then I just couldn't hold it back
and I said "Fuck... fuck. man.

1018
01:35:49.344 --> 01:35:53.414
"I am really. really scared. I really
really. really. really scared right now.

1019
01:35:53.415 --> 01:35:56.082
I don't know what to do. what to think. I..."

1020
01:35:56.083 --> 01:36:02.823
<i>And he said "Daniel. calm down. Calm down.
"Everything will be fine. You are going home.</i>

1021
01:36:02.824 --> 01:36:10.297
<i>"They will come in a few hours or tomorrow.
but one thing is sure Daniel. you will go home".</i>

1022
01:36:10.298 --> 01:36:14.000
Once again it was a weird feeling
because I was sitting there crying

1023
01:36:14.001 --> 01:36:19.406
and making a scene in front of James
and I was about to go home

1024
01:36:19.407 --> 01:36:23.584
and James he didn't have
anything to look forward to.

1025
01:36:24.011 --> 01:36:28.355
<i>James. he went back to
his. his side of the room.</i>

1026
01:36:30.852 --> 01:36:34.421
Ten minutes after they knocked on the door.

1027
01:36:34.422 --> 01:36:39.600
they came in and asked me to put a
blanket over my head and follow them.

1028
01:36:42.764 --> 01:36:48.409
<i>That was the last time I... I saw James.</i>

1029
01:37:08.390 --> 01:37:13.026
I call on my friends. family. and loved
ones to rise up against my real killers.

1030
01:37:13.027 --> 01:37:14.261
the U.S. government.

1031
01:37:14.262 --> 01:37:19.900
For what will happen to me is only a result
of their complacency and criminality.

1032
01:37:19.901 --> 01:37:22.369
I wish I had more time.

1033
01:37:22.370 --> 01:37:25.872
I wish I could have the hope of freedom
and seeing my family once again.

1034
01:37:25.873 --> 01:37:28.449
But that ship has sailed.

1035
01:37:29.511 --> 01:37:34.154
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> I hadn't heard
Jim's voice in... two years.</i>

1036
01:37:35.650 --> 01:37:40.186
You know. I guess. um... you can see
it in his neck. in his face he's just...

1037
01:37:40.187 --> 01:37:43.196
<i>this strength he has at that moment.</i>

1038
01:37:45.092 --> 01:37:50.764
And. um... I think of myself I'd be calling
for my mother or something.

1039
01:37:50.765 --> 01:37:54.568
Um... just the strength he had and I... I... um...

1040
01:37:54.569 --> 01:38:01.915
I... I wanted to feel what he felt
was the reason I watched it.

1041
01:38:04.879 --> 01:38:08.282
I kept getting messages saying.
"John. are you okay?

1042
01:38:08.283 --> 01:38:11.384
John. are you okay about what they
said. about what your brother said?"

1043
01:38:11.385 --> 01:38:13.287
I'm like. "What are you talking about?"

1044
01:38:13.288 --> 01:38:18.532
I call on my brother John. who
is a member of the US Air force.

1045
01:38:19.326 --> 01:38:21.728
Think about what you are doing.

1046
01:38:21.729 --> 01:38:27.240
Think about the lives you destroy.
including those of your own family.

1047
01:38:29.137 --> 01:38:36.216
Even though he was reading that script.
he seemed defiant... to the end.

1048
01:38:37.011 --> 01:38:41.715
I mean. for sure. I still have
some guilt regarding...

1049
01:38:41.716 --> 01:38:47.421
me... and just me and...
unfortunately my profession.

1050
01:38:47.422 --> 01:38:52.258
but I'm sure that he endured torture.

1051
01:38:52.259 --> 01:38:58.332
Interestingly. John and I have... we've
argued a lot about things since that.

1052
01:38:58.333 --> 01:39:03.870
We've been communicating a lot more.
we've come a lot closer as a result of it.

1053
01:39:03.871 --> 01:39:07.340
<i>and I think Jim would have loved that.</i>

1054
01:39:07.341 --> 01:39:08.809
And you know Jim as well as I do.

1055
01:39:08.810 --> 01:39:11.344
he wouldn't have said those things
if there wasn't someone else

1056
01:39:11.345 --> 01:39:16.289
that was going to be harmed as a result
of it. he never cared about himself.

1057
01:39:17.419 --> 01:39:21.654
<i><font color=#FF551>KATIE:</font> My only thought when I found out
about Jim was. "How am I gonna get home?"</i>

1058
01:39:21.955 --> 01:39:25.492
<i>I had missed the initial chaos
of when it all happened.</i>

1059
01:39:25.493 --> 01:39:29.530
<i>but slowly we all kind of came together.</i>

1060
01:39:29.531 --> 01:39:33.099
We literally chose the smallest
room possible in our house.

1061
01:39:33.100 --> 01:39:35.709
But everybody was just kinda huddled together.

1062
01:39:37.339 --> 01:39:41.915
Jim always found a way to bring
people together. Always.

1063
01:39:44.912 --> 01:39:51.485
<i><font color=#FF551>DIANE:</font> I really don't think I. came
to know Jim as a man. as an adult.</i>

1064
01:39:51.486 --> 01:39:55.395
I came to know him through his friends.

1065
01:39:57.559 --> 01:40:03.437
<i>It's hard as a mother. you know. they're
always your children somehow...</i>

1066
01:40:04.632 --> 01:40:07.601
and it's hard to see that they're adults

1067
01:40:07.602 --> 01:40:11.978
with heir own gifts in their
own way in the world.

1068
01:40:13.808 --> 01:40:16.009
<i>The day before his memorial.</i>

1069
01:40:16.010 --> 01:40:20.780
I was like. "shit".

1070
01:40:20.781 --> 01:40:25.251
I was like. now I get it. Now I get it.

1071
01:40:25.252 --> 01:40:30.290
You know. jokingly. I was always
like. you know Jim get a job.

1072
01:40:30.291 --> 01:40:33.927
Jim. you know. save for retirement.

1073
01:40:33.928 --> 01:40:38.532
And so what I didn't recognize.
he was trying to teach me.

1074
01:40:38.533 --> 01:40:42.803
John. you need to look outside of yourself.

1075
01:40:42.804 --> 01:40:47.841
John. It's not about physical or monetary things.

1076
01:40:47.842 --> 01:40:51.912
<i>It's about how you act. It's who you teach.</i>

1077
01:40:51.913 --> 01:40:53.880
<i>who you mentor.</i>

1078
01:40:53.881 --> 01:40:59.325
<i>Who's going to remember you? What
are they going to remember about you?</i>

1079
01:41:02.123 --> 01:41:08.794
<i>Jimmy was included in the long line of
journalists who gave their lives to tell the truth.</i>

1080
01:41:08.795 --> 01:41:14.635
We were just totally humbled by the fact that
the committee in Bayeux would nominate him

1081
01:41:14.636 --> 01:41:22.075
<i>to be included on one of the markers with.
2.000 other deceased journalists.</i>

1082
01:41:22.076 --> 01:41:25.518
<i>It was a very important moment.</i>

1083
01:41:26.881 --> 01:41:34.887
This was Jim. and. uh... it made me
feel very warm inside that. um.

1084
01:41:34.888 --> 01:41:37.597
he was accepted as one of them.

1085
01:41:38.125 --> 01:41:40.367
<font color=#ff00ff>[Didier Speaking]</font>

1086
01:42:01.015 --> 01:42:02.983
<i><font color=#FF551>JOHN SR.:</font> And that's the difference with Jim.</i>

1087
01:42:02.984 --> 01:42:08.762
He was just a you. and a me.
friendly guy. until tested.

1088
01:42:10.592 --> 01:42:14.500
<font color=#ff00ff>[Voice Breaking]</font> Where that
came from I don't know. Brian.

1089
01:42:17.231 --> 01:42:21.667
Because you can't... just can't
grit something like that out.

1090
01:42:21.668 --> 01:42:27.681
You have to have an inner
strength... to do that.

1091
01:42:28.910 --> 01:42:32.518
<i>We discovered Jim just like the world did.</i>

1092
01:42:34.649 --> 01:42:37.558
<i>He was truly a man for others.</i>

1093
01:42:40.988 --> 01:42:45.559
<i><font color=#FF551>NICOLE:</font> Witnessing Jim's murder this
publicly. It sends a message to all of us.</i>

1094
01:42:45.560 --> 01:42:50.796
and my... I guess response to that is.

1095
01:42:50.797 --> 01:42:57.938
<i>having lost so many friends and knowing that
people are purposely out for journalists now...</i>

1096
01:42:57.939 --> 01:43:03.749
<i>I mean. we have to fight back
with our pictures and our words.</i>

1097
01:43:04.745 --> 01:43:07.653
I just... I don't wanna let them win.

1098
01:43:09.416 --> 01:43:13.419
<i>The extent to which the
media coverage took off.</i>

1099
01:43:13.420 --> 01:43:16.289
it was just staggering. It
was absolutely staggering.

1100
01:43:16.290 --> 01:43:22.462
<i>Something like 94% of Americans
were aware of Jim's death</i>

1101
01:43:22.463 --> 01:43:26.833
and how he died. It's the event
with the second most recognition

1102
01:43:26.834 --> 01:43:29.802
in recent American history after 9/11.

1103
01:43:29.803 --> 01:43:34.807
He would have been horrified by that. I mean.
he was there to talk about the Syrian people

1104
01:43:34.808 --> 01:43:37.183
<i>and this is the takeaway.</i>

1105
01:43:40.281 --> 01:43:44.785
<i><font color=#FF551>BRIAN:</font> What is your response to people who
would say that Jim shouldn't have been there?</i>

1106
01:43:44.786 --> 01:43:49.855
<i><font color=#FF551>CLARE:</font> My response is. do you read
the newspaper. do you watch TV?</i>

1107
01:43:49.856 --> 01:43:54.060
<i>You're depending on someone
to bring you that information.</i>

1108
01:43:54.061 --> 01:43:59.032
If you care about what's going on in Syria.
you don't have the right to be like.

1109
01:43:59.033 --> 01:44:00.266
"Oh. why's he there?"

1110
01:44:00.267 --> 01:44:04.177
How do you even know what
"there" is? Because he told you.

1111
01:44:08.942 --> 01:44:13.947
<i><font color=#FF551>PIERRE:</font> I remember the video.
and there was a desert...</i>

1112
01:44:13.948 --> 01:44:18.919
<i>and in the background you could
see that the desert stopped.</i>

1113
01:44:18.920 --> 01:44:23.289
<i>And there was all the valley of Euphrates.</i>

1114
01:44:23.290 --> 01:44:30.763
So I called into what I know from
James. I'm like 100% sure

1115
01:44:30.764 --> 01:44:37.143
<i>that even though he was convinced he
will be killed. he enjoyed the view.</i>

1116
01:44:40.074 --> 01:44:45.745
<i><font color=#FF551>NICOLAS:</font> He died as a free man.
This is not the death of a hostage.</i>

1117
01:44:45.746 --> 01:44:51.084
And that is the difference eventually
between... between Jim and myself.

1118
01:44:51.085 --> 01:44:54.127
I ended up being released...

1119
01:44:55.089 --> 01:44:59.299
but. uh... he ended up free.

1120
01:45:03.965 --> 01:45:06.855
<i>And when we came to James' funeral.</i>

1121
01:45:06.856 --> 01:45:10.470
everything started to become
real again in a way.

1122
01:45:10.471 --> 01:45:14.040
The fact that I managed to say
goodbye to James together with

1123
01:45:14.041 --> 01:45:20.180
<i>all his loved ones was really powerful. It
reminded me how important it was</i> that...

1124
01:45:20.181 --> 01:45:23.756
That James. he... he gave that letter to me.

1125
01:45:31.292 --> 01:45:35.729
<i><font color=#FF551>MICHAEL:</font> "Dear family and friends. I
remember going to the mall with Dad...</i>

1126
01:45:35.730 --> 01:45:38.564
<i>"a very long bike ride with Mom.</i>

1127
01:45:38.565 --> 01:45:43.336
<i>"I remember so many great family times
that take me away from this prison.</i>

1128
01:45:43.337 --> 01:45:48.281
<i>"Dreams of family and friends take me
away and happiness fills my heart.</i>

1129
01:45:49.443 --> 01:45:54.414
<i>"I know you are thinking of me and
praying for me and I am so thankful.</i>

1130
01:45:54.415 --> 01:45:59.920
<i>"I feel you all especially when I pray. I pray
for you to stay strong and to believe.</i>

1131
01:45:59.921 --> 01:46:04.430
<i>"I really feel I can touch you in even
in this darkness when I pray.</i>

1132
01:46:09.263 --> 01:46:11.965
<i>"I think a lot about my brothers and sister.</i>

1133
01:46:11.966 --> 01:46:14.801
<i>"I remember playing werewolf
in the dark with Michael</i>

1134
01:46:14.802 --> 01:46:17.711
<i>"and so many other adventures.</i>

1135
01:46:19.173 --> 01:46:22.108
<i>"I think of chasing Matty and
T around the kitchen counter.</i>

1136
01:46:22.109 --> 01:46:24.310
<i>"it makes me happy to think of them.</i>

1137
01:46:24.311 --> 01:46:26.479
<i>"If there is any money left in my bank account.</i>

1138
01:46:26.480 --> 01:46:29.722
<i>"I want it to go to Michael and Matthew.</i>

1139
01:46:30.217 --> 01:46:32.385
<i>"I'm so proud of you. Michael.</i>

1140
01:46:32.386 --> 01:46:35.021
<i>"and thankful to you for
happy childhood memories.</i>

1141
01:46:35.022 --> 01:46:38.598
<i>"and to you and Kristie for happy adult ones.</i>

1142
01:46:42.029 --> 01:46:45.999
<i>"And big John. how I loved visiting
you and Cress in Germany.</i>

1143
01:46:46.000 --> 01:46:48.001
<i>"Thank you for welcoming me.</i>

1144
01:46:48.002 --> 01:46:51.938
<i>"I think a lot about Ro-Ro and
try to imagine what Jack is like.</i>

1145
01:46:51.939 --> 01:46:54.908
<i>"I hope he has Ro-Ro's personality."</i>

1146
01:46:54.909 --> 01:46:59.946
<i><font color=#FF551>PRIEST:</font> So Mark and Casey. what
name have you given your son?</i>

1147
01:46:59.947 --> 01:47:04.124
- James Michael Foley.
- James Michael Foley.

1148
01:47:04.185 --> 01:47:08.855
<i>"And Mark. so proud of you too. bro.</i>

1149
01:47:08.856 --> 01:47:12.425
<i>"I think of you on the west coast and hope
you're doing some snowboarding and camping.</i>

1150
01:47:12.426 --> 01:47:15.862
<i>"I especially remember us going to
the comedy club in Boston. together.</i>

1151
01:47:15.863 --> 01:47:21.374
<i>"and our big hug after. The special
moments keep me hopeful.</i>

1152
01:47:24.271 --> 01:47:29.542
<i>"Katie. so very proud of you. You were
the strongest and best of us all.</i>

1153
01:47:29.543 --> 01:47:33.546
<i>"I think of you working so hard.
helping people as a nurse.</i>

1154
01:47:33.547 --> 01:47:38.624
<i>"I pray I can come to your wedding.
Now I'm sounding like Grammy.</i>

1155
01:47:40.187 --> 01:47:45.158
<i>"Grammy. please take your medicine.
take walks and keep dancing.</i>

1156
01:47:45.159 --> 01:47:47.828
<i>"I plan to take you out to Margarita's
when I get home.</i>

1157
01:47:47.829 --> 01:47:52.933
<i>"Stay strong because I am going to
need you to help reclaim my life.</i>

1158
01:47:52.934 --> 01:47:55.175
<i>"Jim."</i>

1159
01:48:10.251 --> 01:48:13.219
<i># If I should close my eyes #</i>

1160
01:48:13.220 --> 01:48:16.890
<i># That my soul can see #</i>

1161
01:48:16.891 --> 01:48:19.259
<i># And there's a place at the table #</i>

1162
01:48:19.260 --> 01:48:22.963
<i># That you save for me #</i>

1163
01:48:22.964 --> 01:48:25.899
<i># So many thousand miles #</i>

1164
01:48:25.900 --> 01:48:29.302
<i># Over land and sea #</i>

1165
01:48:29.303 --> 01:48:33.006
<i># I hope today #</i>

1166
01:48:33.007 --> 01:48:37.210
<i># That you hear my prayer #</i>

1167
01:48:37.211 --> 01:48:43.890
<i># And somehow I'll be there #</i>

1168
01:48:45.019 --> 01:48:51.324
<i># It's but a concrete floor
where my head will lay #</i>

1169
01:48:51.325 --> 01:48:57.497
<i># And though the walls of this
prison are as cold as clay #</i>

1170
01:48:57.498 --> 01:49:04.337
<i># But there's a shaft of light
where I count my days #</i>

1171
01:49:04.338 --> 01:49:08.074
<i># So don't despair #</i>

1172
01:49:08.075 --> 01:49:12.245
<i># At the empty chair #</i>

1173
01:49:12.246 --> 01:49:18.819
<i># Somehow I'll be there #</i>

1174
01:49:18.820 --> 01:49:21.587
<i># Some days I'm strong #</i>

1175
01:49:21.588 --> 01:49:25.058
<i># Some days I'm weak #</i>

1176
01:49:25.059 --> 01:49:30.570
<i># And days I'm broken I can barely speak #</i>

1177
01:49:31.032 --> 01:49:33.533
<i># That place in my head #</i>

1178
01:49:33.534 --> 01:49:36.970
<i># Where my thoughts still roll #</i>

1179
01:49:36.971 --> 01:49:43.749
<i># Somehow I've come home #</i>

1180
01:49:44.378 --> 01:49:50.616
<i># And when the winter comes
and the trees lie bare #</i>

1181
01:49:50.617 --> 01:49:56.957
<i># And you just stare out the window
and the darkness there #</i>

1182
01:49:56.958 --> 01:50:03.729
<i># Well. I was always late for
every meal you swear #</i>

1183
01:50:03.730 --> 01:50:07.539
<i># But keep my place #</i>

1184
01:50:08.102 --> 01:50:12.405
<i># On the empty chair #</i>

1185
01:50:12.406 --> 01:50:19.084
<i># And somehow I'll be there #</i>

1186
01:50:20.081 --> 01:50:28.593
<i># And somehow I'll be there #</i>

